1 NOTE Saga2, 218. Han døde 1103 i Roma, på veg til Istanbul me d 5 skip som
2 CONT omkom.
1 NOTE Lendermann.
2 CONT 860751442. Lendemann Skofte Agmundsen GISKE (20443) (20444) was born in 1040 in Giske. He was a Lendemann in 1066. (20445) (20446) Deltok i kong Magnus Barfots store ferd til Orkenøene 1098. He died in 1103 in Roma (I).(20447) Han
2 CONC  var borsønn av Eystein Orre og omtales etter olav Kyrres død som en eldre mann som hadde voksne sønner og i lengre tid hadde bodd på familiesetet Giske. Han hadde 1101-1102 en alvorlig tvist med sin frende Magnus Barfot om noe d
2 CONC annefæ som begge tilegnet seg. Retten synes å ha vært på Skoftes side, men magnus ville ikke gi seg. Skogte seilte derfor 1102 med 5 velutrustede skip med sine 3 sønner Finn, Agmund og Tord, drog til Flanderen, våren 1103 til F
2 CONC rankrike og om sommeren til Gibralter og Rom hvor handøde. He was married to Sigrid TORDSDTR.
1 NOTE Lendmann. Levde 1050.
2 CONT
2 CONT Skofte var brorsønn til Eystein Orre. I 1066 var han lendermann og omtales etter Olav Kyrres død i 1093 som en eldre mann. Hans sønner var da voksne og han hadde i lengre tid bodd på familiesetet Giske.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han deltok i kong Magnus Barfots store ferd til Orknøyene i 1098. I 1101-1102 hadde han en alvorlig tvist med sin frende Magnus Barfot om noe dannefæ som begge tilegnet seg. Retten synes å ha vært på Skoftes side, men Magnus ville
2 CONC  ikke gi seg. Skofte seilte derfor fra landet i 1102 med 5 velutrustede skip sammen med sine 3 sønner, Finn, Agmunn og Tord. De dro til Flandern, våren 1103 til Frankrike og om sommeren til Gibraltar og Roma hvor han døde.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Snorre Sturlasson: Magnus Barefoot's Saga:
2 CONT
2 CONT "12. DEATH OF THE EARLS OF ORKNEY.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT Skopte Ogmundson, a grandson of Thorberg, was a gallant lenderman, who dwelt at Giske in Sunmore, and was married to Gudrun, a daughter of Thord Folason. Their children were Ogmund, Fin, Thord, and Thora, who was married to Asolf 
2 CONC Skulason. Skopte's and Gudrun's sons were the most promising and popular
2 CONT men in their youth."
2 CONT
2 CONT "19. OF THE QUARREL OF KING MAGNUS AND SKOPTE.
2 CONT Skopte Ogmundson came into variance with King Magnus, and they quarrelled about the inheritance of a deceased person which Skopte retained; but the king demanded it with so much earnestness, that it had a dangerous appearance. Man
2 CONC y meetings were held about the affair, and Skopte took the resolution that he and his son should never put themselves into the king's power at the same time; and besides there was no necessity to do so. When Skopte was with the ki
2 CONC ng he represented to him that there was relationship between the king and him; and also that he, Skopte, had always been the king's friend, and his father's likewise, and that their friendship had never been shaken. He added, "Peo
2 CONC ple might know that I have sense enough not to hold a strife, sire, with you, if I was wrong in what I asked; but it is inherited from my ancestors to defend my rights against any man, without distinction of persons." The king was
2 CONC  just the same on this point, and his resolution was by no means softened by such a speech. Then Skopte went home."
2 CONT
2 CONT "20. FIN SKOPTASON'S PROCEEDINGS.
2 CONT Then Fin Skoptason went to the king, spoke with him, and entreated him to render justice to the father and son in this business. The king answers angrily and sharply. Then said Fin, "I expected something else, sire, from you, than
2 CONC  that you would
2 CONT use the law's vexations against me when I took my seat in Kvaldinsey Island, which few of your other friends would do; as they said, what was true, that those who were left there were deserted and doomed to death, if King Inge had
2 CONC  not shown greater generosity to us than you did; although many consider that we brought shame and disgrace only from thence." The king was not to be moved by this speech, and Fin returned home."
2 CONT
2 CONT "21. OGMUND SKOPTASON'S PROCEEDINGS.
2 CONT Then came Ogmund Skoptason to the king; and when he came before him he produced his errand, and begged the king to do what was right and proper towards him and his father. The king insisted that the right was on his side, and said
2 CONC  they were "particularly impudent."
2 CONT
2 CONT Then said Ogmund, "It is a very easy thing for thee, having the power, to do me and my father injustice; and I must say the old proverb is true, that one whose life you save gives none, or a very bad return. This I shall add, that
2 CONC  never again shall I come into thy service; nor my father, if I can help it." Then Ogmund went home, and they never saw each other again."
2 CONT
2 CONT "22. SKOPTE OGMUNDSON'S VOYAGE ABROAD.
2 CONT The spring after, Skopte Ogmundson made ready to travel out of the country. They had five long-ships all well equipped. His sons, Ogmund, Fin, and Thord, accompanied him on this journey. It was very late before they were ready, an
2 CONC d in autumn they went over to Flanders, and wintered there. Early in spring they sailed westward to Valland (France), and stayed there all summer. Then they sailed further, and through Norvasund (Straits of Gibraltar); and came in
2 CONC  autumn to Rome, where Skopte died. All, both father and sons, died on this journey. Thord, who died in Sicily, lived the longest. It is a common saying among the people that Skopte was the first Northman who sailed through Norvas
2 CONC und; and this voyage was much celebrated."
1 NOTE He was District Judge and Revenue Officer, named in the records in 1094. He lived at Giske, Sunnmøre, Norway.
1 NOTE Skofte Ogmundsson eller Agmundsson, også kjent som Skofte fra Giske (død 1103) var en norsk lendmann av Giske-ætten som på begynnelsen av 1100-tallet lot utruste en korsferd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Skofte var sønn av Ogmund Torbergsson, gift med Gudrun Tordsdatter, datter av Tord Foleson, og far til sønnene Ogmund, Finn og Tord, og døtrene Tora og Ragnhild. Tora ble gift med Åsolv Skuleson på Rein.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sammen med sine tre sønner dro Skofte omkring 1101 ut på korsferd. De seilte gjennom Gibraltarstredet (Njørvasund) med fem skip, men 1103 døde alle de fire lederne på reisen, og deres følgesvenner, som samme år kom frem til Pal
2 CONC estina, vendte snart tilbake. Omtrent samtidig (10. juli 1103) var den danske kongen Erik Eiegod død på Kypros på vei til Det hellige land. Den neste korsferden som utgikk fra Skandinavia var kong Sigurd Jorsalfares i 1107.
1 NOTE He was District Judge and Revenue Officer, named in the records in 1094. He lived at Giske, Sunnmøre, Norway.
1 NOTE Fra Per Tuff's bok om Losna-ætta :
2 CONT s.115
2 CONT Tord Folessons dattersønn var Skofte Ågmundsson av Arnmødlingeætten og vår første korsfarer. Både Skofte 
2 CONT Ågmundsson og hans tre sønner døde som før nevnt under korsferden. Den fjerde sønn, Pål Skoftesson 
2 CONT (eller Pål på Aure) ble igjen hjemme på Giske og forplantet slekten videre. Pål hadde to døtre, Ingebjørg, som 
2 CONT ble bestemor , og Sigrid, som ble oldemor til Arne Korsbrekke, som igjen var far til ridder Erlend Arnesson, nevnt 
2 CONT 1295.
