2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT nge the Younger
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Inge the Younger was King of Sweden in the early 12th century and the son of king Halsten[1][2] and he was probably Halsten's youngest son.[2] According to unreliable traditions[1] Inge would have ruled together with his brother P
2 CONC hilip Halstensson after the death of their uncle Inge the Elder:[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hallstein's sons were Philip and Ingi, and they succeeded to the Kingdom of Sweden after King Ingi the elder. (The 13th century Hervarar saga)[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Other sources say that after the death of Philip (1118), Inge the Younger was the sole king of Sweden,[1] but the year of his own death is unknown.[2] According to the regnal list in the Westrogothic law, Inge was poisoned with an
2 CONC  evil drink in Östergötland:[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Niunði war Ingi konongær, broðher Philipusær konongs, oc heter æptir Ingæ kononge, Halstens konongs brødhær. Hanum war firigiort mæð ondom dryk i Østrægøtlanði, oc fek aff þy banæ. Æn Sweriki for e wæl, mædhæn þer frænlingær rædhu
2 CONC .[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT The tenth (Christian king) was king Inge, the brother of king Philip, and he was named after king Inge (the Elder). He was killed by evil drink in Östergötland and it was his bane. But Sweden fared forever well, while these kinsme
2 CONC n ruled.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known whether Inge was still alive when the Norwegian king Sigurd I of Norway invaded Småland in 1123,[2] but when Inge died, it was the end of the House of Stenkil.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Inge is reported to have been married with Ulfhild who was the daughter of the Norwegian Haakon Finnsson and who would later marry the Danish king Nils Svensson and even later the Swedish king Sverker the Elder.[2]
2 CONT
