2 CONT
2 CONT =='''Ulv Galiciefarer'''==
2 CONT
2 CONT Ulv Galiciefarer (also known as Galicieulv (Galiciwolf) c. 1010), aka Jarl Galizur-Ulfric was a Danish jarl, a Viking chieftain who became famous for his raids, looting and pillaging the lands of Galicia in the early eleventh cent
2 CONC ury, perhaps in 1028 or 1048, during the reign of Bermudo III and Ferdinand I of Leon. According to Saxo Grammaticus and the Knýtlinga saga, Galicieulv was grandfather of Boedil Thurgotsdatter, the wife of poEric I of Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Knýtlinga saga is one of the main sources on his life. It states that he left "bravely with his westward to conquer Jakobsland" (the land of Santiago de Compostela). Most likely he was devoted to raiding and plundering with re
2 CONC latively little opposition. Some legends claim that he served as a mercenary for galician count Rodrigo Romániz, in the assault on Labio Castle, near present-day Lugo, over the riots against Bermudo III. Ulf also helped Romariz ag
2 CONC ainst the Basque, achieving victory. The raids continued until Ulf was expelled from Cresconio troops, but not before looting Redondela monastic communities as on the islands of San Simon, Cies and Toralla enclosures. After his ex
2 CONC pulsion, the Viking attacks on Galicia fell sharply.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Saxo Grammaticus, Úlfr (Old Norse: Wolf) was of noble family. It is speculated that he was the son of Ulf the Earl.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married when young with Bothild Håkonsdatter (b. 1014), daughter of the Earl of Lade Håkon Eiriksson. They had a son, Thurgot Fagrskinna Ulfsen (n. 1032, nicknamed ’pale skin’ although apparently his skin was dark). Turgot woul
2 CONC d become Count Palatine (Leibhauptman des Königs) Turgot's daughter, Bothild Thorgunnsdatter (Bothilda, 1068-1134) married the Danish King Eric I of Denmark. The couple died on a pilgrimage to the Holy Land. However, his son, Prin
2 CONC ce Lavard became Duke of Schleswig.
2 CONT
