2 CONT * '''Date: 1415 August 3, Place of issue: Vadstena'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Karl Magnusson, magistrate in Tiohärad, sells to his nephew Nils Guse on certain conditions everything that his son Magnus Karlsson inherited from '''Gjord Fot's wife Elin Ragvaldsdotter.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sources''' Original: Or. perg. Bergshammarsamlingen RA 0107.02
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =The hypothesis that seems to have the highest explanatory value - i.e. gives the best answer to unanswered questions, is the following:=
2 CONT
2 CONT I have partly analyzed the data you report and partly identified a number of explicit and implicit boundary conditions in Göran Sparrlöf's article about the Fot family in the Genealogists' Yearbook 2004.
2 CONT As long as Elin Ragvaldsdotter's origin is not documented, one can only synthesize the information one has and formulate a hypothesis that can be tested and questioned
2 CONT .
2 CONT The hypothesis that seems to have the highest explanatory value - i.e. gives the best answer to unanswered questions, is the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hypothesis : Gjurd Fot's wife, Elin Ragvaldsdotter, was a daughter of Ragvald Erengislesson Puke, sister of Peter Ragvaldsson Puke/Farmer and of an unknown sister, married with Karl Magnusson Oxpanna.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the same reason as Sparrlöf - source criticism - I have chosen to ignore previous hypotheses. I have also chosen to ignore the question of what weapon she was carrying for the time being. Test of the hypothesis based on Sparrö
2 CONC f's article is solely based on relationships: ("The answer is in the relationships")
2 CONT
2 CONT • p. 222 "Then Nils Uddsson and Per Germundsson handed over their farm in Graby to Gjurd Fot..."'Explanation': Nils Uddsson's wife, Ingeborg Erengrisledotter, was, according to the hypothesis, the aunt of Gjurd's wife, Elin Ragval
2 CONC dsdotter. Per Germundsson was according to Raneke (p. 724) son of Gjurd (Sic).
2 CONT
2 CONT • p. 223 "...the known donations that he (Gjurd Fot) and Elin Ragvaldsdotter made to Nydala monastery and to other church institutions often consisted of farms owned by her." 'Explanation': ...son Peter owes his father's debt to N
2 CONC ydala monastery... [EÄ I : 514]
2 CONT
2 CONT • p. 226 "Elin Ragvaldsdotter apparently had a family relationship of some kind to earl Erengisle Sunesson (boat)"
2 CONT 'Explanation': According to the hypothesis was that Elin and Erengisle Sunesson were cousins ​​after Ragvald Puke.
2 CONT
2 CONT • p. 226 note 22 "Of particular interest is the morning gift letter, which the knight Harald Karlsson (Stubbe) issued from his seat farm Ålhult in Södra Vi parish, Sevede härad, 1398 11/6. In this, Gjurd Fot is listed among the fa
2 CONC sts (SRP no. 2917). Harald Karlsson was related to earl Erengisle Sunesson (boat)…” 'Explanation': Harald Karlsson's father, Karl Haraldsson was cousin to Erengisle Sunesson after Jon Pedersen Baad (boat).
2 CONT
2 CONT • p. 227 "There are also some people who hypothetically could have been related to Elin Ragvaldsdotter, but it remains to be tested whether this was the case. In Rääf's record collection, a woman named Sigrid Ragvaldsdotter is men
2 CONC tioned. In 1378, she donated land in Änga in Västra Ryd parish in Ydre härad to Linköping Cathedral. Among those who sealed the donation letter is earl Erengisle Sunesson.” 'Explanation': Sigrid R, could be Elin's sister, and marr
2 CONC ied to Karl Magnusson (Oxpanna) whose son Magnus inherited cropland from Elin Ragvaldsdotter.
2 CONT
2 CONT • p. 228 "Finally, Peter Ragvaldsson can be mentioned, who in 1399 sealed Elin Ragvaldsdotter's and Gjurd Fot's donation letter to Nydala monastery. Raneke suspects that Peter was Elin's brother, but his coat of arms is unknown. '
2 CONC Explanation': Completely as hypothesized.
2 CONT
2 CONT • p. 229 "It can also be mentioned that Elin Ragvaldsdotter left an inheritance to Magnus Karlsson, who was the son of the lawman Karl Magnusson (ox pan with horns) in Småland." 'Explanation': See hypothesis above, p. 227.
2 CONT
2 CONT • p. 233 "About Gjurd Fot's son, Erengisle Gjurdsson i Graby, there is only a few pieces of information and we currently do not have any certain knowledge about which of the father's wives was his mother . Palmskiöld announces tha
2 CONC t he was the son of Elin Ragvaldsdotter, but the information is unconfirmed." 'Explanation': Ernegisle Gjurdsson was, based on the hypothesis, most likely named after his mother's postulated grandfather, Erengisle Petersson (Farme
2 CONC r).
2 CONT
2 CONT With this, I have tested the hypothesis based on a number of implicit boundary conditions, and find that it has a relatively high explanatory value - thus not saying that it is true, only that it is more likely than other hypothes
2 CONC es with lower explanatory value...
2 CONT
2 CONT In my post above, I avoided addressing the question of which weapon Elin Ragvaldsdotter carried - for good reason - as it is very easy to jump to conclusions based on the similarity of weapons. F. island a weapon may have been att
2 CONC ributed to a person who does not belong to the family at all.
2 CONT
2 CONT I still have to address the question, as it is in principle of decisive importance for determining family affiliation.
2 CONT
2 CONT The problem is that so far I have not found any primary source where it is explicitly stated that Elin sealed the document - and that the seal is preserved and unequivocally identified! If anyone has access to such a source, I wou
2 CONC ld be grateful for that information!
2 CONT
2 CONT Raneke gives her shield as 'cleft, l. emerging cleft demi-eagle' [p. 732]. She is listed as known in 1399, which can be interpreted as the year referring to the basis for determining her coat of arms - which in this case probably 
2 CONC refers to the undated document SDHK 15155 - issued by Elin R. attached (addition to?) SDHK 15154 in 1399.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=15155&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=2&postid=sdhk_15155&tab=post#tab SDHK no: 15155]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1399 July 28 *'''
2 CONT
2 CONT (Elin Ragnvaldsdotter) regarding the aforementioned (in SDHK no. 15154 ) estate being hers after her husband Bengt Magnusson and his children
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sources:''' Original:  Or. perg. RA 0101  images, Letter text Thessin forscrifno godz ærw min rætto afflynga godz æptir my husband Bänikt magnosson ok his children... Printed regest RPB 2996,Literature and commentary
2 CONT The letter is attached (addendum?) SDHK no 15154 (13990728).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=15155&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&page=2&postid=sdhk_15154&tab=post#tab SDHK no: 15154]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1399 July 28''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elin Ragnvaldsdotter and her husband Gjurd Fot transfer for debt to Vadstena kloster properties in Linderås and Frinnaryds parishes.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sources:''' Original:  Or. perg. RA 0101  images, Post-medieval copies:  Peringskiöld: E 60: E fol. 175 (reg); Örnhielm: IX p. 996 Printed regest RPB 2996 Literature and commentary. An ode. letter (SDHK no. 15155 ) attached: 
2 CONC "Thessin forscrifno godz ärw min röto afflynga godz äptir min husbonda bänckt magnosson ok hans barn".
2 CONT
2 CONT =The main message in SDHK 15155 is that Elin Ragnvaldsdotter=
2 CONT
2 CONT The main message in SDHK 15155 is that Elin R. disposes of crop goods after her first husband, Bengt Magnusson and his children. This means that all of them were dead, but that at least one child survived their father, which is wh
2 CONC y Elin inherited all their property as a back inheritance and had free disposal rights over it.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are five seal bags on the letter, of which at least two contain visible seals (see image below). Seal no. 2 must have been the seal attributed to Elin R. of Raneke, as it can probably be interpreted as a cleft half eagle – a
2 CONC  weapon also attributed to a Mattias Bryniolfsson (k. 1385) [Raneke, p. 924], who sold land in Ydre to Bo Jonsson (Grip) and also held property in Asby sn. (Bryniolfsson's coat of arms is described by Rääf [part 1, no. 52] as: "..
2 CONC .an indistinct erect right-facing lizard or some other animal.")
2 CONT
2 CONT Interesting are the coat of arms sketches above seals 3 - 5. They obviously belong to:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 3) '''Nils Magnusson (Röde )''' k. 1374 – 1427. [J Raneke, p. 31]
2 CONT
2 CONT * 4) '''Sten Haraldsson (Stubbe)''' k. 1376 – 1417. [J Raneke, p. 72]
2 CONT
2 CONT * 5) '''Sten Lalasson''' k. 1390 – 1420. [J Raneke p. 427].
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils Magnusson's sister, Christina, was married to Sten Lalasson who had a daughter, Elin, who was married to Filip Bonde - Elin Ragvaldsdotter's uncle I postulated - brother of Ragvald Erengislesson!
2 CONT
2 CONT The Sigillants obviously belonged to the same geosocial cluster as the Bonde/Puke family and ensured Elin Ragvaldsdotter's (Puke?) rights.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Gjurd Karlsson is not mentioned in the letter (SDHK 15155), it seems (?) seal 1) belongs to Elin R rather than seal 2) as she is the issuer - but I am not completely sure. Could it be that seal 2) instead belongs to Mattias Bry
2 CONC niolfsson? You can perhaps sense a couple of hanging feathers in the seal with an angle that is close to Mattia's coat of arms - or?!
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000212116607861&size=large}} 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Det medeltida Sverige Band 4 SMÅLAND: 6 Ydre härad
2 CONT * The Swedish Diplomatarium's main catalog of medieval letters
