2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Name:''' '''Johann Herbst'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Variations of the name:''' Herfst, Herbest, Herbster, Erbst
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Origin of the name:''' The Herbst surname is derived from the German word “Herbst”, meaning autumn. It has been suggested that the surname may have originally been used by people whose main work occurred during the fall harvest
2 CONC . For example, Herbster, most likely meant “grape harvester.” First found in Bavaria, where the name was anciently associated with the tribal conflicts of the area. Early records include a Marquart Herbst, in Würzburg in 1220. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birth date:''' circa 1664
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birth Place:''' Bremen, Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Arrival in South Africa:''' 1686
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Date of Death:''' circ 1728
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Place of Death:''' South Africa
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Parents:''' Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Partner 1:''' Cecilia van Angola
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''b1 Angenitie''' = 1685
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Partner 2:''' Lijsbeth Sanders van de Kaap
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''b2 Gerbrecht''' = 1.10.1702
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Occupation & Career:''' In 1686 overseer of the Comp.'s cattle post in Hottentots Holland, later (1690) burgher at Drakenstein.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''HERBST JOHANN'''. - Bremen. In 1686 overseer of the Comp.'s cattle post in Hottentots Holland, later (1690) burgher at Drakenstein. + after 1728. A son, Johannes (2.9.1685) [this is a mistake - there is no such entry], by Cicil
2 CONC ia of Angola, two daughters by Lysbeth (Sanders) of the C.: Clara ~ Jan Harmensz Potgieter, Gerbergt (1.10.1702) ~ Johannes Vosloo. (V.R.S., vol. 12, p. 41; MR. Vrye Lieden of 1690; CJ 1125: 234, will in Stellenbosch Arch., vol. 6
2 CONC 49: 11; Inv. O.C. 2: 102; Test. O.C. 4: 147; MOOC 14/26 nr. 14.)
2 CONT
2 CONT - Dr. J. Hoge, Personalia of the Germans at the Cape, 1652-1806, Archives Year Book for South African History (Cape Town: Government Printer, Union of South Africa, 1946)
2 CONT
2 CONT *Birth Date  1664  
2 CONT *Death Date  1728  
2 CONT *First Name  Johann  
2 CONT *Last Name  Herbst  
2 CONT *Suffix  SV/PROG  
2 CONT *Gender  Male  
2 CONT *Birth Location  Bremen, Deutschland  
2 CONT *Death Location  Cape Colony 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1699 Johann Herbst received a grant of land on the upper reaches of the Wamakers River, in the newly settled region of Wagenmakersvalleij (now Wellington). This was the year that Lijsbeth would have been freed from the chain-ga
2 CONC ng, if her fine was not paid. Herbst (or Herfst) named his farm ‘Opperherfst’. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived there with Lijsbeth (now known as Lijsbeth Sanders)138 until his death in 1734, though in 1724 the farm was transferred to his son-in-law, Johannes Vosloo, on condition that Vosloo maintain him for the remainder of his li
2 CONC fe (the agreement with Vosloo made no mention of Lijsbeth Sanders).
2 CONT
2 CONT The genealogical registers tell us that he had two daughters with Lijsbeth: Clara Herfst, who married Johannes Harmensz Potgieter in 1712 and Gerbrecht Herfst, who married Johannes Vosloo.
2 CONT
2 CONT When they first settled at Opperherfst, Lijsbeth and Herbst were assisted by the burgher (and tailor) Heinrich Venter, who agreed to help Herbst, ‘wegen zijn swaekheijt’ (on account of his infirmity) to establish the farm. They wo
2 CONC uld sow and reap together and share the profit and the loss.
2 CONT
2 CONT Venter made his 60 cattle available for ploughing and manure and pastured his sheep on the land. In 1704, however, Venter received his own land, further downstream on the Berg River, and he presumably left Herbst and Lijsbeth Sand
2 CONC ers to fend for themselves.
2 CONT
2 CONT In time, as we shall see, they gathered around themselves a small community of relatives, including the husbands and lovers of Lijsbeth’s daughters by Louis of Bengal.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT See the article by Susie Newton-King:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sodomy, race and respectability in Stellenbosch and Drakenstein, 1689 – 1762: the story of a family, loosely defined
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/photo.php/7/7c/Jurgen-9.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT This is an intriguing story about the fate of Gerrit Coetsé who was exectued on 22 September 1733. He was the grandson of Lijsbeth through her daughter Elisabeth Louisz by freed slave Louis van Bengal. It provides a good look at t
2 CONC he strained lives of Lijsbeth and her family as they struggled to fit into Cape Huguenot/European society. Lijsbeth's own story begins on page 18, and Jan Herbst on page 22. 
