1 OCCU Farmer at Kliprivier dist Swellendam 1776-1814
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Born in the farm Voorhuis, district Swellendam. Baptized 25 December 1753. Married Barbara Wilhelmina Vogel 16 March 1777. Had 11 children of which the fourth was their only son Hermanus / Hermanus Frederik
2 CONT
2 CONT Was 'burger' of Swellendam, heemraad of Swellendam, deacon of the NG Church in Swellendam
2 CONT
2 CONT Was brother of President Hermanus Steyn, who was born ten years before him.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Connection between Johannes Steyn and the Kliprivier Farm
2 CONT EH Burrows describes Kliprivier thus: '... fiannly, the materipeice, Klip River, an epitome of Cape Dutch architecture, one one of the most gracious homesteads in the country.' p.61, Overberg Outspan.
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.artefacts.co.za/main/Buildings/bldgframes.php?bldgid=7480 we have the following:
2 CONT Kliprivier. This is certainly the finest house in the Overberg, overlooking the Bruintjes River valley. Its association with the Steyns goes back a long way. In 1725 Jan Loots, one of the first five heemraden of the drostdy, held 
2 CONC the land on loan. Alter his death in 1745 the lessee became Anthonie Lombard, a son of the Huguenot Pierre Lombard. From him it passed to his son of the same name, whose wife, Isabella Potgieter, was a sister-in-law of Hermanns St
2 CONC eyn d'Oude. In 1769 Lombard died and his widow married Ch. Gottlieb Lessing, having paid off accumulated debt on the farm, left it in 1771 and returned to Stellenhosch. In 1776 the farm was re-let to Johannes Sleyn. born in 1753 o
2 CONC n the farm Voorhuis. This man was a younger brother of the 'President' of the Swellendam republic, and both were sons of Hermanus d'Oude.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes Steyn must have spent most of his life on Kliprivier, very possibly in the old house of Jan Loots, until about 1812 he decided to build a new one. This new house is shown on HS Schutte's diagram, done in 1816, and there a
2 CONC ppears as a T-shaped house. In 1814 the farm - still a loan-farm - passed from Johannes Steyn to his son Hermanus Frederik; and then, owing to quarrels about the lease of part of the land (now known as Bellevue), to the assistant 
2 CONC landdrost Frouenvelder; the latter was sent to 'Het Warme Bad', now Caledon, in the same capacity and wanted compensation for the buildings he had put up on Bellevue. The landdrost maintained that the terms of his lease compelled 
2 CONC him to demolish all buildings on leaving the farm. This quarrel went on for years, and governor Cradock cut the Gordian knot by withdrawing Steyn's lease and putting the whole farm up for sale. The buyer at once re-sold the farm t
2 CONC o Marthinus Steyn (a first cousin of Hermanus Steyn) who held it until his death in 1834, and his widow held it for a few years longer.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was almost certainly Marthinus Steyn who enlarged the house to its present size and gave it an H-plan by adding back wings. He also added the front-gable, probably about 1833. It consists of a split pediment crowned by a segmen
2 CONC tal cap, as is sometimes seen on cupboards of the time. The straight, pointed sides with their clefts can also be seen as a development born from a misunderstanding of winged volutes. Van der Meulen sees it as a version of the ear
2 CONC lier Lutheran Church facade, which had a 'draped' split pediment with a spire in the centre. But the end-gable of Dieprivier. Caledon, which is related to Kliprivier but earlier, does not call to mind the Lutheran Church at all. A
2 CONC  suggested attribution to LM Thibault (who did work in Swellendam) is open to doubt, although of course his influence may have lingered.
2 CONT
2 CONT The gable has magnificent plaster decoration. Two, and probably three, periods of plasterwork can be seen, the higher being by far the best. The heavy pergola columns, a rare feature at the time, are of slightly later vintage for 
2 CONC some of its overhead irons actually pierce the swags over the windows.
2 CONT
2 CONT The facade woodwork is all original, belonging to the original T-shaped house. There is a good drop-fanlight, but all the shutters are internal, and no signs exists of there ever having been external shutters. Inside, the screen h
2 CONC as disappeared, though the beams, broad ceiling planks and the doors remain. The back wings are obviously later in date, and there is no back gable, only a dormer gable, with an opening in it to which a flight of steps leads up. K
2 CONC liprivier is a very large house, with two rooms beside the voor- en agterkamer in each of the four wings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later owners of the farm were the Reids, the Reitzes, and others. They all seem to have accepted the house as it was (except for the pergola), so that it is singularly unspoilt. Now in use as a guest-house, it is once again in goo
2 CONC d nick.
2 CONT [Fransen p463.]
2 CONT
2 CONT These notes were last edited on 2025 03 21
2 CONT
2 CONT Writings about this entry
2 CONT Fransen, Hans. 2004. The old buildings of the Cape. A survey of extant architecture from before c1910 in the area of Cape Town - Calvinia - Colesberg - Uitenhage. Johannesburg & Cape Town: Jonathan Ball Publishers. pg 463
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Steyn owners of Kliprivier dist Swellendam according to EH Burrows in his book Overberg Outspan. Johannes Steyn b1c3d12 loan farmer 1776 - 1814  
2 CONT Hermanus Steyn b1c3d12e4 loan farmer January - July 1814 
2 CONT Marthinus Steyn b1c8d13 owner 1815 - 1837
2 CONT
2 CONT
