1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Maria KICKERS ==
2 CONT * Born: ca. 1660, Amsterdam (exact parentage unknown)  
2 CONT * Died: after 1723, Cape of Good Hope  
2 CONT
2 CONT === Marriages/ Other Known Relationship  ===
2 CONT 1. '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Jan-Cornelisz/4141861275630023780 Jan Cornelisz van Oud-Beijerland]'''  
2 CONT * Married: 11 July 1683, Cape Town (NGK) [https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3QS7-89ML-49Y7-J FS record]  
2 CONT * Divorce: 22 January 1700, Cape of Good Hope (Council of Justice, CJ 3-22, fol. 77) [http://www.e-family.co.za/ffy/p7773.htm transcript]  granted on grounds of Jan’s impotence and Maria’s admitted relationship with Friedrich Both
2 CONC .  
2 CONT * Jan was permitted to remarry; Maria was not.  
2 CONT * Jan died in 1713.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Ferdinandus-Appel-b2/6000000001580940776 Ferdinandus APPEL]'''  
2 CONT * Affair produced son '''Theunis''', confirmed by DNA [http://repository.up.ac.za/bitstream/handle/2263/32007/Greeff_Appel(2013).pdf study by Greeff & Erasmus, 2013].  
2 CONT * Allegations of his paternity were already made in 1689 by two women who alleged that Theunis, Maria's oldest child, was fathered by Ferdinandùs Appel.  In divorce proceedings (1700) and the adultery case (16 Nov 1706) she declar
2 CONC ed all her children were fathered by '''Friedrich BOTH(A)''', not her legal husband.    Modern DNA evidence shows that her eldest son '''Theunis''' was indeed fathered by '''Ferdinandus APPEL'''.    
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Friedrich-Botha-SV-PROG/5356428241620125193 Friedrich BOTH(A)]''' (aka Boot, Bot), of Wagenheim/Wageningen  
2 CONT * Cohabited with Maria ca. 1687 onwards; several children born before marriage.  
2 CONT * Married: 21 June 1714, Stellenbosch [https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:S3HT-6LX3-V8P FS record]  
2 CONT * Court records:  
2 CONT ** 1706 convicted with Maria of adultery, assault, and cattle theft.  
2 CONT ** Sentenced to 10 years’ banishment to Mauritius, but returned after 2 years.  
2 CONT * In court (1706) Maria testified she and Botha had sworn vows privately and lived as man and wife.  
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221120485985&size=large}}
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000123663725823 ''''Appel Botha Cornelitz : the abc of a three hundred year old divorce case'.''' Jaco M. Greeff & J. Christoff Erasmus Department of Genetics, University of Pretoria
2 CONC , Pretoria 0002, South Africa.]
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children (all surnamed Botha) ===
2 CONT 1.'''[https://www.geni.com/people/Theunis-Botha/6000000005788458301 Theunis Botha]''' (b. bef. 15 Apr 1686)  
2 CONT * Biological father: Ferdinandus Appel (DNA confirmed).  
2 CONT * Baptised as son of Botha.  
2 CONT * Ancestor of Pres. Louis Botha and Pres. P.W. Botha.  
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000221121053824&size=large}}
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000123663725823 ''''Appel Botha Cornelitz : the abc of a three hundred year old divorce case'.''' Jaco M. Greeff & J. Christoff Erasmus Department of Genetics, University of Pretoria
2 CONC , Pretoria 0002, South Africa.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Willem-Botha/6000000005788630146 Willem Botha]'''  
2 CONT * Father presumed Friedrich Botha.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.'''[https://www.geni.com/people/Jacobus-Botha/6000000005788692627 Jacobus Botha]'''  
2 CONT * Father presumed Friedrich Botha.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Johannes-Botha/6000000005788776995 Johannes Botha]'''  
2 CONT * Father presumed Friedrich Botha.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Catharina-Botha/6000000005788883829 Catharina Botha]'''  
2 CONT * Father uncertain (likely Botha).  
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Cornelia-Botha/6000000005788932905 Cornelia Botha]'''  
2 CONT * Paternity disputed (Botha or Appel).  
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Maria-Botha/6000000005789017971 Maria Botha]'''  
2 CONT * Father Friedrich Botha (per testimony).  
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Anna-Botha/6000000005789059171 Anna Botha]'''  
2 CONT
2 CONT * Bapt. ca. Mar 1702 Stellenbosch, recorded as daughter of Botha.  
2 CONT === Notes ===
2 CONT * She is sometimes confused with Maria Kikkert, alleged partner of Jan Coenraadsz Visser in SAG. This is incorrect.  
2 CONT * mtDNA Haplogroup H confirmed from descendants.  
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sources & References ===
2 CONT * [https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3QS7-89ML-49Y7-J NGK Cape Town marriage, 11 July 1683]  
2 CONT * [http://www.e-family.co.za/ffy/p7773.htm Divorce proceedings, Council of Justice, 22 Jan 1700 (CJ 3-22, fol. 77)]  
2 CONT * Crim. & Civ. 1701–1708, 16 Nov 1706, p. 47 (Cape Archives – no public digital copy known)  
2 CONT * [https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:S3HT-6LX3-V8P Marriage to Botha, Stellenbosch, 21 Jun 1714]  
2 CONT * [http://databases.tanap.net/cgh/ Council of Policy record, 13 Sep 1718, TANAP C.47, pp. 32–42 (refers to “Maria Bombam”)]  
2 CONT * [http://repository.up.ac.za/bitstream/handle/2263/32007/Greeff_Appel%282013%29.pdf DNA study: Jaco Greeff & Erasmus, "Appel-Botha-Cornelitz: the abc of a 300-year-old paternity case" (2013)]  
2 CONT * [https://www.familytreedna.com/public/CapeDutch/default.aspx?section=mtresults FamilyTreeDNA Cape Dutch Project – mtDNA Haplogroup H results]  
2 CONT *[https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000123663725823 ''''Appel Botha Cornelitz : the abc of a three hundred year old divorce case'.''' Jaco M. Greeff & J. Christoff Erasmus Department of Genetics, University of Pretori
2 CONC a, Pretoria 0002, South Africa.]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT == Narrative Biography of Maria KICKERS ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria KICKERS (born ca. 1660, Amsterdam – died after 1723, Cape of Good Hope) was a woman of uncertain parentage whose life at the Cape was both eventful and unusually well-documented in the legal records of the Dutch East India C
2 CONC ompany (VOC).  
2 CONT
2 CONT === Marriage to Jan Cornelisz ===
2 CONT On 11 July 1683 Maria married [https://www.geni.com/people/Jan-Cornelisz/4141861275630023780 Jan Cornelisz van Oud-Beijerland] at Cape Town’s Dutch Reformed Church ([https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3QS7-89ML-49Y7-J FS 
2 CONC record]). According to Cape custom, she did not assume her husband’s surname; her surname was never “Botha.”  
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage quickly became troubled. By Maria’s own later testimony, her husband was impotent and even encouraged her to have children with another man, [https://www.geni.com/people/Friedrich-Botha-SV-PROG/5356428241620125193 Fri
2 CONC edrich Botha] (also known as Boot or Bot). 
2 CONT
2 CONT >''On today the 13 May 1689 Sworn to before me the undersigned Sibrandus Mancadan secretary to Stellenbosch at the request of Belijtie Fredericks wife of the freeburger Harmen Jansen from Noorthorn dwelling at Stellenbosch, the ho
2 CONC nourable Hendrik Cornelis and the honourable Catrijn Cloeten in the presence of the undernamed witnesses admitting and declaring and attesting true and true to be, that she, the attestator, during the pregnancy of Maria Kickers (n
2 CONC ow four years in the past) from the mouth of the aforesaid Maria Kickers had heard, that the child with which she was pregnant, did not belong to her husband Jan Cornelis but to Frederik Boot, using in essence these words: that sh
2 CONC e did not belong to all men, but kept herself to one, and that Frederik Boot was the father of the child with which she was now pregnant, and also the father of her first child was that of Ferdinandus Appel. In similar fashion was
2 CONC  declared by Beatrix Gijsberts declaring that at this same time the 11 August 1685 (it was a Sunday to the attestator's best knowledge) likewise from the aforesaid Maria Kickers to have heard, that Frederick Boot had known her, an
2 CONC d that she from that time on had become pregnant''.[Notarial Deeds: Declaration, 12.8.1699]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the divorce proceedings of 22 January 1700 she openly declared that none of her children had been fathered by her legal husband, but all by Botha ([http://www.e-family.co.za/ffy/p7773.htm transcript of CJ 3-22, fol. 77]). The c
2 CONC ourt accepted this testimony, dissolved the marriage, and permitted Jan to remarry. Maria, however, was not given leave to remarry, which would later complicate her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Relationship with Friedrich Botha ===
2 CONT From at least 1687 Maria was living with Friedrich Botha, and by the turn of the century they had a large family together. Muster rolls of 1701 list Botha in Stellenbosch with eight children, while Maria appears separately in the 
2 CONC same district, suggesting they were officially recorded apart.  
2 CONT
2 CONT On 16 November 1706 Maria and Botha were convicted of cohabitation, which still counted as adultery since Maria had not been given leave to remarry after her divorce. Court records state that she defended their union by saying the
2 CONC y had sworn vows to each other in private and had lived as man and wife for years, raising eight children together (Crim. & Civ. 1701–1708, p. 47). Both were sentenced to ten years’ banishment to Mauritius for adultery, assault, a
2 CONC nd cattle theft, though they were back at the Cape within two years.  
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her first husband Jan in 1713, Maria and Friedrich were finally able to marry formally. Their marriage took place at Stellenbosch on 21 June 1714 ([https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:S3HT-6LX3-V8P FS re
2 CONC cord]).
2 CONT
2 CONT === Affair with Ferdinandus Appel ===
2 CONT While most of Maria’s children were fathered by Botha, one son — [https://www.geni.com/people/Theunis-Botha/6000000005788458301 Theunis Botha] (b. before 15 April 1686) — was fathered by [https://www.geni.com/people/Ferdinandus-Ap
2 CONC pel-b2/6000000001580940776 Ferdinandus Appel]. This was suspected as early as 1689, when two women accused Maria of bearing Appel’s child. Recent DNA evidence has confirmed the claim: patrilineal descendants of Theunis share the s
2 CONC ame Y-DNA haplogroup as Appel, not Botha ([http://repository.up.ac.za/bitstream/handle/2263/32007/Greeff_Appel%282013%29.pdf Greeff & Erasmus 2013 study]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT This discovery means that many famous South Africans, including President Louis Botha and President P.W. Botha, actually descend from Appel rather than Botha on their direct male line.  
2 CONT
2 CONT === Later Years and Records ===
2 CONT In 1718 Maria is probably the “Maria Bombam” referred to in a Council of Policy resolution, which noted her family’s debts (TANAP C.47, pp. 32–42; [http://databases.tanap.net/cgh/ TANAP database]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her youngest child, Anna, was baptised at Stellenbosch in March 1702 as a daughter of Botha. The fate of Maria after 1723 is unclear, but she was still alive in the early 18th century.  
2 CONT
2 CONT === Notes on Identity ===
2 CONT Maria has often been confused with “Maria Kikkert,” the supposed partner of Jan Coenraadsz Visser, but there is no evidence connecting the two women. She also could not have been one of the “orphan girls” sent from Rotterdam in 16
2 CONC 88, since she was already at the Cape at the time of her marriage in 1683.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mtDNA, tested from descendants in the FamilyTreeDNA Cape Dutch Project, belongs to Haplogroup H, the most common maternal lineage in Europe ([https://www.familytreedna.com/public/CapeDutch/default.aspx?section=mtresults Projec
2 CONC t results]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT === Legacy ===
2 CONT Although legally married to Cornelisz until 1700, and only formally married to Botha in 1714, Maria spent most of her adult life with Friedrich Botha, raising a large family at Stellenbosch. The complex parentage of her children —
2 CONC  some fathered by Botha, at least one by Appel — has been clarified only in modern times by DNA studies.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her story highlights both the difficulties of life at the early Cape and the unusual openness of the surviving court records, which capture in detail her voice, her testimony, and her determination to live as she chose.  
2 CONT ----
