2 CONT
2 CONT HEINRICH, son of WELF I Duke of Bavaria & his second wife Judith de Flandre ([1074]-Ravensburg 13 Dec 1126, bur Weingarten).  The Annalista Saxo records the death in 1126 of "Heinricus dux Bawarie…filius Welphonis ducis…[et] Iudhi
2 CONC tam"[335].  On the death of his father-in-law in 1106, he inherited extensive territories in Saxony around Lüneburg and in the Bardengau[336].  He was appointed to succeed his brother in 1120 as HEINRICH IX "der Schwarze" Duke of 
2 CONC Bavaria.  He initially supported the candidacy of his son-in-law Friedrich Duke of Swabia to succeed Emperor Heinrich V as King of Germany in 1125, but eventually supported the election of Lothar von Süpplingenberg, presumably on 
2 CONC the understanding of his son's marriage to Lothar's only daughter, which took place in 1127[337].  The enmity of the Staufen family which this created was to have far-reaching consequences in Germany for the rest of the century.  
2 CONC Duke Heinrich became a monk at Weingarten shortly before his death[338].  The Historia Welforum records that Duke Heinrich became a monk shortly before he died "in castro Ravenspurch"[339].  The necrology of Lüneburg records the d
2 CONC eath "12 Dec" of "Henricus dux"[340].  The necrology of Weingarten records the death "Id Dec" of "Heinricus dux et m n c …pater Welfonis, hic sepultus"[341]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m WULFHILD of Saxony, daughter of MAGNUS Duke of Saxony [Billung] & his wife Sofia of Hungary (-Altdorf 29 Dec 1126, bur Weingarten).  The Annalista Saxo names "Wifhildem et Eilicam" as the two daughters of Duke Magnus & his wife,
2 CONC  specifying that Wulfhild married "Heinrico duci, filio Welfi ducis senioris de Bawaria"[342].  The Historia Welforum records that Wulfhild died at Altdorf "decimo sexton die post mortem mariti" and was buried "in monasterio sanct
2 CONC i Martini"[343].  The necrology of Lüneburg records the death "29 Dec" of "Wlfhild ducissa"[344].  The necrology of Weingarten records the death "IV Kal Jan" of "Wuolfhildis ducissa hic sepulta"[345]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): ---.  The name of Duke Heinrich's mistress is not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Heinrich & his wife had seven children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.             HEINRICH ([1108]-Quedlinburg 20 Oct 1139, bur Königslutter).  The Annalista Saxo names "Heinricum inclitum ducem Saxonie et Bawarie et Welfonem et quatuor filias" as children of Duke Heinrich & his wife Wulfhild[346
2 CONC ].  He succeeded his father in 1126 as HEINRICH X "der Stolze" Duke of Bavaria.  His father-in-law installed him as administrator of the lands previously held by Matilda of Tuscany, after conceding papal ownership of them in retur
2 CONC n for a usufruct[347].  His father-in-law invested him as HEINRICH Duke of Saxony in 1137 shortly before his death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)             HEINRICH ([1132/33]-Braunschweig 6 Aug 1195, bur Braunschweig Cathedral).  He succeeded in 1142 as HEINRICH "der Löwe" Duke of Saxony, and in 1156 as HEINRICH XII Duke of Bavaria.  He lost Saxony and Bavaria 1180, b
2 CONC ut kept his territories in Braunschweig. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -        DUKES of SAXONY. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.             KONRAD (-Bari 17 Mar 1126, bur Molfetta).  The Historia Welforum names (in order) "Counradum, Heinricum, Guelfonem" as the three sons of "Heinricus dux ex Wulfilde", specifying that Konrad "in clericum ordinatus" an
2 CONC d became a monk at "Clarevallense monasterium", visited Jerusalem, and died on his return journey at Bari where he was buried[348].  Cistercian monk.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.             SOPHIE (-10 Jul before 1147).  The Historia Welforum names (in order) "Iuditham, Sophiam, Mahtildem, Wulfildem" as the four daughters of "Heinricus dux ex Wulfilde", specifying that Sophie married firstly "Bertoldus
2 CONC  dux de Zaringen" and secondly "Leopaldus marchio de Stira"[349].  The Annalista Saxo names "Heinricum inclitum ducem Saxonie et Bawarie et Welfonem et quatuor filias" children of Duke Heinrich & his wife Wulfhild, specifying that
2 CONC  Sophie (named second in the list of daughters) married firstly "Bertoldus dux de Zaringe" and secondly "marchio Liuppoldus de Stire"[350].  The Genealogia Zaringorum names "Sophya soror Heinrici ducis Saxonie" as wife of "Berchto
2 CONC ldus", specifying that they were childless and that she married secondly "comiti de Stire"[351].  She became a nun at Admont after her second husband died.  The necrology of Admunt records the death "VI Id Jul" of "Sophya ex march
2 CONC ionissa cv"[352].  The necrology of Weingarten records the death "V Id Jul" of "Suophia com de Stira hic sepulta"[353].   m firstly BERTHOLD III Herzog von Zähringen, son of BERTHOLD II Herzog von Zähringen [Baden] & his wife Agne
2 CONC s von Rheinfelden (-killed in battle near Molsheim 3 Dec 1122, bur St Peter im Schwarzwald).   m secondly ([1122/23]) LEOPOLD “der Starke” Markgraf of Styria, son of OTAKAR II Markgraf of Styria & his wife Elisabeth of Austria [Ba
2 CONC benberg] (-24 Oct 1129). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.             JUDITH (after 1100-22 Feb [1130/31], bur Walburg im Heiligen Forst, Alsace).  The Historia Welforum names (in order) "Iuditham, Sophiam, Mahtildem, Wulfildem" as the four daughters of "Heinricus dux ex Wulfilde", sp
2 CONC ecifying that Judith married "Friderico Suevorum duci"[354].  The Annalista Saxo names "Heinricum inclitum ducem Saxonie et Bawarie et Welfonem et quatuor filias" as children of Duke Heinrich & his wife Wulfhild, specifying that o
2 CONC ne of the daughters (mentioned first in the list of daughters, but not named) married "Fridericus dux Suevorum"[355].   m ([1119/21]) as his first wife, FRIEDRICH II “der Einäugige” Duke of Swabia, son of FRIEDRICH I Duke of Swabi
2 CONC a [Staufen] & his wife Agnes of Germany (1090-Alzey 4 or 6 Apr 1147, bur Walburg Abbey). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.             MATHILDE (-16 Feb or 16 Mar [1183], bur Kastl).  The Historia Welforum names (in order) "Iuditham, Sophiam, Mahtildem, Wulfildem" as the four daughters of "Heinricus dux ex Wulfilde", specifying that Mathilde marrie
2 CONC d firstly "Theopaldo filio Theopaldi marchionis de Voheburch" and secondly "Gebehardo de Sulzbach"[356].  The Annalista Saxo names "Heinricum inclitum ducem Saxonie et Bawarie et Welfonem et quatuor filias" as children of Duke Hei
2 CONC nrich & his wife Wulfhild, specifying that "Quarta Machtildis nomine" married firstly "Thieppoldo iuniori, filio Thieppoldi marchionis senioris" and secondly "Gebehardus filius Beringeri comitis de Sulzbach"[357].   m firstly ([11
2 CONC 28]) DIEPOLD [IV] Markgraf von Vohburg, son of DIEPOLD [III] von Giengen Markgraf von Vohburg & his first wife Adelajda of Poland (-[1128]).   m secondly (contract 24 Oct 1129) GEBHARD [III] Graf von Sulzbach, son of BERENGAR [I] 
2 CONC Graf von Sulzbach & his second wife Adelheid von Wolfratshausen [Diessen] (-28 Oct [1188], bur Kastl). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.             WELF [VI] ([16 Dec 1114/15 Dec 1116]-Memmingen 14/15 Dec 1191, bur Steingaden).  The Annalista Saxo names "Heinricum inclitum ducem Saxonie et Bawarie et Welfonem et quatuor filias" as the children of Duke Heinrich 
2 CONC & his wife Wulfhild[358].  The Historia Welforum names (in order) "Counradum, Heinricum, Guelfonem" as the three sons of "Heinricus dux ex Wulfilde"[359].  After the death of his older brother Heinrich in 1139, Welf led the opposi
2 CONC tion to Konrad III King of Germany in Bavaria, although he was defeated at Weinsberg in 1140[360].  He founded Kloster Steingaden in 1147.  He joined the Second Crusade with King Konrad in 1147, and took part in the 25 Oct 1147 de
2 CONC feat at Dorylaeum[361].  When King Konrad agreed an alliance with Emperor Manuel I against Roger II King of Sicily, Welf agreed to support the Sicilians[362].  Welf was heavily defeated at Flochberg near Nördlingen in Feb 1150 and
2 CONC  reached a peace settlement with King Konrad in 1151[363].  In 1152, Frederick I "Barbarossa" King of Germany invested Welf as Duke of Spoleto and Marchese of Tuscany, and with Sardinia and the lands formerly held by Matilda Ctss 
2 CONC of Tuscany, as a means of acquiring Welf support after his election[364].  Vogt von Zwiefalten 1152.  Von Ravensburg 1152.  Short of money, Welf gradually returned his Italian lands to Emperor Friedrich I, and from 1173 ceased to 
2 CONC use his Italian titles.  Welf VI also agreed to make Emperor Friedrich his successor in the Welf lands around Ravensburg[365].  The necrology of Raitenbuch records the death "XIX Kal Jan" of "dux Guelfo VI fundator monasterii Stai
2 CONC ngadensis"[366].  The necrology of Weingarten records the death "XVIII Kal Jan" of "Welfo dux, Welfonum ultimus filius Heinrici ducis"[367].  m (before Jan 1133) UTA von Calw Herzogin von Schauenburg, daughter of GOTTFRIED I Graf 
2 CONC von Calw Pfalzgraf am Rhein & his wife Liutgard von Zähringen (-1196).  The Annales Sindelfingenses name (in order) "Uotam [uxorem Welphoni de Spoleto] et Lutgardam" as daughters of "Uotæ"[368].  The Historia Welforum names "filia
2 CONC m Gotefridi…palatine de Kalwe, Outam" as wife of "Guelfo…frater…Heinrico duce"[369].  She founded Kloster Allerheiligen in 1192.  Duke Welf & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)             WELF [VII] (-Siena 12 Sep 1167, bur Steingaden).  The Annales Sindelfingenses name "Welphonem iuniorem" as son of "Welphoni de Spoleto" and his wife Uta, specifying that he besieged Tübingen "1165 VIII Id Sep"[370].
2 CONC   Duke of Spoleto 1160.  He died of malaria while fighting on Emperor Friedrich I's Italian expedition of 1167[371].  The Hugonis Ratisponensis Cronica records the death in 1167 of "Welfo filius Welfonis" during the emperor's Ital
2 CONC ian campaign[372].  The necrology of Weingarten records the death "II Id Sep" of "Welf dux iunior"[373]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.             WULFHILD (-18 May after 1156).  The Historia Welforum names (in order) "Iuditham, Sophiam, Mahtildem, Wulfildem" as the four daughters of "Heinricus dux ex Wulfilde", specifying that Wulfhild married "Roudolfus Breg
2 CONC antinus comes"[374].  The Annalista Saxo names "Heinricum inclitum ducem Saxonie et Bawarie et Welfonem et quatuor filias" as the children of Duke Heinrich and his wife Wulfhild, specifying that "terciam nominee Wifhildem" married
2 CONC  "Rodolfus comes de Bregence"[375].  Nun at Wessobrunn 1155.   m as his second wife, RUDOLF Graf von Bregenz, son of ULRICH [X] Graf von Bregenz & his wife Bertha von Rheinfelden (-27/28 Apr 1160). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Heinrich had one illegitimate child by Mistress (1): 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.              ADALBERT (-1144).  Abbot of Corvey.  The Annalista Saxo records that "Adalbero frater Heinrici ducis" succeeded as Abbot of Corvey in 1138[376].  It is assumed that "Heinrici ducis" refers to Heinrich X [Welf] who 
2 CONC was duke of Bavaria and Saxony at that date.  Adalbert is unlikely to have been the legitimate son of Duke Heinrich IX as he is not named as such in other sources (see above) which appear to provide an exclusive list of the duke's
2 CONC  children by his wife Wulfhild.  
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Heinrich IX Herzog von Bayern (1) M, #7962, b. circa 1074, d. 13 December 1126 Last Edited=15 Jul 2005 Consanguinity Index=0.02%
2 CONT      Heinrich IX Herzog von Bayern was born circa 1074. He was the son of Guelph IV Herzog von Bayern and Judith de Flandre. (1) He married Wolfhildis von Sachsen, daughter of Magnus I Herzog von Sachsen and Sophia of Hungary, bet
2 CONC ween 1095 and 1100.  He died on 13 December 1126.
2 CONT      Heinrich IX Herzog von Bayern also went by the nick-name of Heinrich 'the Black'. (1) He gained the title of Herzog von Sachsen auf der Elbe. He succeeded to the title of Herzog von Bayern in 1120. (1) Children of Heinrich IX
2 CONC  Herzog von Bayern and Wolfhildis von Sachsen -1. Conrad von Bayern d. 17 Mar 1126 -2. Maud von Bayern d. 16 Mar 1183 -3. Wolfhilde von Bayern d. a 1160 -4. Judith von Bayern+ b. c 1100, d. a 1130 -5. Heinrich X Herzog von Bayern+
2 CONC  b. c 1102, d. 20 Oct 1139 -6. Guelph VI von Bayern, Duca di Spoleto+ b. c 1115, d. 15 Dec 1191 -7. Sophie von Bayern b. b 1126, d. 11 Jul 1145
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Wikipedia: Heinrich IX. (Bayern) aus Wikipedia, der freien Enzyklopädie Wechseln zu: Navigation, Suche Heinrich mit Wulfhild in der Historia Welforum
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich der Schwarze (* 1075; † 13. Dezember 1126 in Ravensburg) war als "Heinrich IX." Herzog von Bayern. Er kam aus dem Haus der Welfen und war ein Sohn Welfs IV. und Bruder Welfs V., nach dessen nachfolgerlosem Tode er 1120 da
2 CONC s Herzogtum Bayern übernahm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Durch seine Ehe mit Wulfhild Billung, der Tochter des letzten Sachsenherzogs Magnus aus dem Hause der Billunger, erlangten die Welfen Besitzungen in Norddeutschland. Die Ehe seiner Tochter Judith mit dem staufischen Herzog Friedri
2 CONC ch II. von Schwaben legte den Grundstein für die späteren welfisch-staufischen Konflikte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eine besondere Rolle spielte Heinrich der Schwarze bei der Königswahl von 1125. Zunächst förderte er die Kandidatur seines Schwiegersohnes Friedrich II. von Schwaben. Kurz vor dem entscheidenden Wahlgang änderte er jedoch seine Me
2 CONC inung und stimmte für den anderen Kandidaten Herzog Lothar von Sachsen, der dadurch als Lothar III. zum deutschen König gewählt wurde. Diese Meinungsänderung wurde offensichtlich ausgelöst durch das Heiratsversprechen von Lothars 
2 CONC Tochter Gertrud mit Heinrichs Sohn Heinrich (der Stolze), das 1127 eingelöst wurde. Die verprellten Staufer riefen danach Friedrichs jüngeren Bruder Konrad zum Gegenkönig aus. Der welfisch-staufische Konflikt nahm seinen Lauf. Fam
2 CONC ilie [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT Aus der zwischen 1095 und 1100 geschlossenen Ehe mit Wulfhild Billung von Sachsen gingen sieben Kinder hervor:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Heinrich der Stolze († 1139), Herzog von Bayern und Sachsen
2 CONT     * Konrad († 1154), Zisterziensermönch
2 CONT     * Judith (* nach 1100, † 1130/31) – verheiratet um 1121 mit Friedrich II., Herzog von Schwaben
2 CONT     * Sophie († um 1145) – verheiratet mit 1. Berthold III. von Zähringen und 2. Markgraf Leopold von Steyr.
2 CONT     * Mathilde, († 1183) – verheiratet mit 1. Markgraf Diepold IV. von Vohburg (Rapotonen) und 2. Graf Gebhard III. von Sulzbach
2 CONT     * Welf VI. (* 1115/16, † 1191), Herzog von Spoleto
2 CONT     * Wulfhild († nach 1160) – verheiratet mit Rudolf Graf von Bregenz
2 CONT
2 CONT Literatur [Bearbeiten]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Sigmund Ritter von Riezler: Heinrich IX.. In: Allgemeine Deutsche Biographie (ADB). Band 11. Duncker & Humblot, Leipzig 1880, S. 461 f.
2 CONT     * Kurt Reindel: Heinrich IX. der Schwarze. In: Neue Deutsche Biographie (NDB). Band 8. Duncker & Humblot, Berlin 1969, S. 343.
2 CONT
