2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin of Wessex, also known as Godwine, Goodwin, Godwyn or Goodwyn (c. 1001–April 15, 1053) was one of the most powerful lords in England under the Danish king Canute the Great and his successors. Canute made him the first Earl o
2 CONC f Wessex. Godwin was the father of King Harold Godwinson and Edith of Wessex, wife of King Edward the Confessor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin's father was probably Wulfnoth Cild who was Thegn of Sussex, although later documents describe his father as a churl. Wulfnoth was supposedly a sixth generation descendant of King Ethelred of Wessex, the older brother of Al
2 CONC fred the Great; Ethelred's descendants were passed over in the royal succession, but became prominent nobles in the kingdom. However this descent of Godwin is still being researched, and should not be taken as certain. Wulfnoth le
2 CONC d a section of the royal fleet into piracy and as a consequence had his lands forfeited, and was exiled. It was left to his young son, Godwin, to improve the family fortunes after his father's death in 1014. In his day, Earl Godwi
2 CONC n was seen as very much of a new man, who had been "made" by two advantageous marriages to Danish noblewomen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin was a major supporter of Edmund Ironside, the son of King Ethelred the Unready. While Edmund was in rebellion against his father, Canute and his army invaded England. Edmund was killed, along with many of his supporters, bu
2 CONC t Godwin survived and pledged his loyalty to Canute. He befriended Canute's brother-in-law, Earl Ulf, and became one of Canute's advisors, accompanying him to Denmark to suppress a rebellion there. By 1018 he was an earl , becomin
2 CONC g Earl of the Wessex in about 1019. In 1022 he married Thyra Sveinsdóttir (also called Thyra Swensdottir) Canute's sister. She died soon afterwards without issue, but Godwin continued to gain prestige and by 1023 he was the most p
2 CONC owerful earl in England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin married a second time to a Danish noblewoman, Gytha Thorkelsdóttir (also known as Githa), said to be the granddaughter of the legendary Viking Styrbjörn Starke and great-granddaughter to Harold Bluetooth, king of Denmark an
2 CONC d thus also ancestor to King Canute. The marriage resulted in the birth of many children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sweyn Godwinson, Earl of Herefordshire (c. 1023-1052). At some point he declared himself an illegitimate son of Canute the Great but this is considered to be a false claim. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Harold II of England (c. 1022-October 14, 1066) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tostig Godwinson, Earl of Northumbria (c. 1026-September 25, 1066) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith of Wessex, (c. 1030-December 19, 1075), queen consort of Edward the Confessor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyrth Godwinson (c. 1030-October 14, 1066) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gunhilda of Wessex, a nun (c. 1035-1080) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ælfgifu of Wessex (c. 1035) 
