2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Godwin of Wessex, also known as Godwine, Goodwin, Godwyn or Goodwyn was one of the most powerful lords in England under the Danish king Canute the Great and his successors. Canute made him the first Earl of Wessex. Godwin was the 
2 CONC father of King Harold Godwinson and Edith of Wessex, wife of King Edward the Confessor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin's father was probably Wulfnoth Cild who was Thegn of Sussex, although later documents describe his father as a churl. Wulfnoth was supposedly a sixth generation descendant of King Ethelred of Wessex, the older brother of Al
2 CONC fred the Great; Ethelred's descendants were passed over in the royal succession, but became prominent nobles in the kingdom. However this descent of Godwin is still being researched, and should not be taken as certain. Wulfnoth le
2 CONC d a section of the royal fleet into piracy and as a consequence had his lands forfeited, and was exiled. It was left to his young son, Godwin, to improve the family fortunes after his father's death in 1014. In his day, Earl Godwi
2 CONC n was seen as very much of a new man, who had been "made" by two advantageous marriages to Danish noblewomen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin was a major supporter of Edmund Ironside, the son of King Ethelred the Unready. While Edmund was in rebellion against his father, Canute and his army invaded England. Edmund was killed, along with many of his supporters, bu
2 CONC t Godwin survived and pledged his loyalty to Canute. He befriended Canute's brother-in-law, Earl Ulf, and became one of Canute's advisors, accompanying him to Denmark to suppress a rebellion there. By 1018 he was an earl , becomin
2 CONC g Earl of the Wessex in about 1019. In 1022 he married Thyra Sveinsdóttir (also called Thyra Swensdottir) Canute's sister. She died soon afterwards without issue, but Godwin continued to gain prestige and by 1023 he was the most p
2 CONC owerful earl in England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin married a second time to a Danish noblewoman, Gytha Thorkelsdóttir (also known as Githa), said to be the granddaughter of the legendary Viking Styrbjörn Starke and great-granddaughter to Harold Bluetooth, king of Denmark an
2 CONC d thus also ancestor to King Canute.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leo: Europäische Stammtafeln, J.A. Stargardt Verlag, Marburg, Schwennicke, Detlev (Ed.), Reference: II 78.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leo: Nachkommen Gorms des Alten, 1978 , Brenner, S. Otto, Reference: page 246.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Godwin, Earl of Wessex From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin of Wessex Reign 	1019–1053 Spouse 	Thyra Sveinsdóttir Gytha Thorkelsdóttir Issue Sweyn, Earl of Herefordshire Harold II, King of England Tostig, Earl of Northumbria Edith, Queen of England Gyrth, Earl of East Anglia Gunhild
2 CONC a Ælfgifu Leofwine, Earl of Kent Wulfnoth House 	House of Godwin Born 	990 Died 	15 April 1053
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin of Wessex (Old English: Gōdwine) (990 – 15 April 1053), was one of the most powerful lords in England under the Danish king Cnut the Great and his successors. Cnut made him the first Earl of Wessex. Godwin was the father of
2 CONC  King Harold Godwinson and Edith of Wessex, wife of King Edward the Confessor. Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Rise: support of Edmund, then Cnut
2 CONT     * 2 Height of power: support of Harold
2 CONT     * 3 Later conflicts, decline, and death
2 CONT     * 4 Children
2 CONT           o 4.1 Family Trees
2 CONT     * 5 In popular culture
2 CONT     * 6 Sources
2 CONT     * 7 Notes
2 CONT     * 8 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT Rise: support of Edmund, then Cnut
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin's father was possibly Wulfnoth Cild who was a thegn of Sussex, although later documents describe his father as a churl.[1] Wulfnoth led a section of the royal fleet into piracy and as a consequence had his lands forfeited, 
2 CONC and was exiled. In his day, Earl Godwin was seen as very much of a new man, who had been "made" by two advantageous marriages to Danish noblewomen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin was a major supporter of Edmund Ironside, the son of King Æthelred the Unready. While Edmund was in rebellion against his father, Cnut and his army invaded England. Edmund was killed, along with many of his supporters, but 
2 CONC Godwin survived and pledged his loyalty to Cnut.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1018 he was an earl, probably of eastern Wessex, and then by around 1020 of all Wessex.[2] Between 1019 and 1023 he accompanied Cnut on an expedition to Denmark, where he distinguished himself, and shortly afterwards married Gy
2 CONC tha, the sister of the Danish earl, Ulf, who was married to Cnut's sister, Estrid.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Height of power: support of Harold
2 CONT
2 CONT On 12 November 1035, Cnut died. His kingdoms were divided among three rival rulers. Harold Harefoot, Cnut's illegitimate son with Ælfgifu of Northampton, seized the throne of England. Harthacnut, Cnut's legitimate son with Emma of
2 CONC  Normandy, reigned in Denmark. Norway rebelled under Magnus the Noble. In 1035, the throne of England was reportedly claimed by Alfred Ætheling, younger son of Emma of Normandy and Æthelred the Unready, and half-brother of Harthac
2 CONC nut. Godwin is reported to have either captured Alfred himself or to have deceived him by pretending to be his ally and then surrendering him to the forces of Harold Harefoot. Either way Alfred was blinded and soon died at Ely.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1040, Harold Harefoot died and Godwin supported the accession of his half-brother Harthacnut to the throne of England. When Harthacnut himself died in 1042 Godwin finally supported the claim of his half-brother Edward the Confe
2 CONC ssor to the throne. Edward was another son of Emma and Æthelred, having spent most of the previous thirty years in Normandy. His reign restored the native royal house of Wessex to the throne of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later conflicts, decline, and death
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his alleged responsibility for the death of Edward's brother Alfred, Godwin secured the marriage of his daughter Edith (Eadgyth) to Edward in 1045. As Edward drew advisors, nobles and priests from his former place of refug
2 CONC e in a bid to develop his own power base, Godwin soon became the leader of opposition to growing Norman influence. After a violent clash between the people of Dover and the visiting Eustace II, Count of Boulogne, Edward's brother-
2 CONC in-law, Godwin was ordered to punish the people of Dover (as he and Leofric, Earl of Mercia had done in Worcester, in Leofric's own earldom). This time, however, Godwin refused, choosing to champion his own countrymen against a (v
2 CONC isiting) foreign ruler and his own king. Edward rightly saw this as a test of power, and managed to enlist the support of Siward, Earl of Northumbria and Earl Leofric. Godwin and his sons were exiled from the kingdom in September 
2 CONC 1051. However, they returned the following year with an armed force, which gained the support of the navy, burghers, and peasants, so compelling Edward to restore his earldom. This however set a precedent to be followed by a rival
2 CONC  earl some years later, and then by Godwin's own son in 1066.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 15 April 1053 Godwin died suddenly, after collapsing during a royal banquet at Winchester. Some colourful accounts claim that he choked on a piece of bread while denying any disloyalty to the king.[4] However this appears to be
2 CONC  later Norman propaganda. Contemporary accounts indicate that he just had a sudden illness, possibly a stroke.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Harold succeeded him as Earl of Wessex, an area then covering roughly the southernmost third of England. With the death of Earl Siward (1055) and later Earl Ælfgar (1062), the children of Godwin were poised to assume sole 
2 CONC control. Tostig was helped into the earldom of Northumbria, thus controlling the north. The Mercian earl was sidelined, especially after Harold and Tostig broke the Welsh-Mercian alliance in 1063. Harold later succeeded Edward the
2 CONC  Confessor and became King of England in his own right. At this point, both Harold's remaining brothers in England were earls in their own right, Harold was himself king and in control of Wessex, and he had married the sister of E
2 CONC arl Edwin of Mercia and Morcar, Earl of Northumbria (who had succeeded his brother Tostig). Godwin's family looked set to inaugurate a new royal dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Sweyn Godwinson, Earl of Herefordshire (c. 1023-1052). At some point he declared himself an illegitimate son of Cnut the Great but this is considered to be a false claim.
2 CONT    2. Harold II of England (c. 1022-14 October 1066)
2 CONT    3. Tostig Godwinson, Earl of Northumbria (c. 1026-25 September 1066)
2 CONT    4. Edith of Wessex, (c. 1030-19 December 1075), queen consort of Edward the Confessor
2 CONT    5. Gyrth Godwinson (c. 1030-14 October 1066)
2 CONT    6. Gunhilda of Wessex, a nun (c. 1035-1080)
2 CONT    7. Ælfgifu of Wessex (c. 1035)
2 CONT    8. Leofwine Godwinson, Earl of Kent (c. 1035-14 October 1066)
2 CONT    9. Wulfnoth Godwinson (c. 1040)
2 CONT
