1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William of Torgar was a son of Ketil Krok and NN Einarsdotter Fluga of Tjøtta. He was most likely born in the first half of the 1070s, after his parents' marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ''Saga of King Ingi'', part of the ''Heimskringla'' by Snorri Sturluson, is the main source to information about William's life. There, Snorri writes that William was a lendman of King Sigurd II of Norway and that he was kille
2 CONC d by Sigurd Slembe, a pretender to the throne, in 1139.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born c. 1179, around forty years after the death of William, Snorri Sturluson wrote the following about William in the Heimskringla, authored between c. 1220 and c. 1235:
2 CONT
2 CONT 3em>''Thereafter Sigurd came south to Vikar [Vik in Brønnøy], and seized King Sigurd's lendermen, William Skinnare and Thorald Kept, and killed them both.''
2 CONT
2 CONT 3em>''(Sigurd styrede da syd til Vikar, og der tog de Viljalm Skinnare, — han var kong Sigurds lendermand — og Toralde Kjeft og dræbte dem begge.)''
2 CONT
2 CONT William is believed to be named in honour of his relative King William of England (the Conqueror). It is unknown why the sagas call him 'skinnare' (skinner).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''LITERATURE'''
2 CONT
2 CONT - Heber, Gustav 1944. Norsk adel. https://urn.nb.no/URN:NBN:no-nb_digibok_2018052948501
