2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT "Scotland was then ruled by Thorfinn in the northern districts and MacBeth in the southern districts. Malcolm, Duncan's eldest son, rebelled twice against MacBeth in an effort to gain the throne. His grandfather, Crinan, was slain
2 CONC  in 1045 near Dunkeld "with nine times twenty heroes" as he led an aborted attempt to put his grandson on the throne. The second attempt was more successful as Malcolm, at the head of an English Saxon army defeated and killed MacB
2 CONC eth while his Norwegian allies were engaged elsewhere and Malcolm ascended the throne in 1057 as King Malcolm III Ceann Mor (Canmore). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1068, Malcolm took as his second wife, Margaret, later known and revered as St. Margaret of Scotland. She had fled England with her brother Edgar Aetheling after the Norman Conquest. During Malcolm's 37 year reign, the first ev
2 CONC ents now known as Highland Games were held on the Braes of Mar to choose the best available men to serve as his servants and soldiers. His death in battle in December 1093 and the death of his wife, several days later brought on a
2 CONC  turbulent time which saw Malcolm's eldest son, King Duncan II murdered by Malcolm's brother Donald Bane, Lord of the Isles, in order to become king. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Another son, Edgar, finally secured the throne in 1097 with the help of another English army of Saxons and Normans led by his mother's brother, Edgar Aetheling. King Malcolm III's hereditary possessions devolved on his youngest br
2 CONC other, Maelmare, the first celtic Earl of Atholl and on his death, the earldom passed to Malcolm III's namesake, the second son of his first marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Malcolm III., called Canmore (Ceanmohr, or Great-head), King of Scotland, was eldest son of Duncan, who was murdered by Macbeth in 1039. After Duncan's death Malcolm fled for safety to his kinsman, Siward, Danish Earl of Northumbe
2 CONC rland, and continued to live for many years in England. In 1054 Siward, with the sanction of Edward the Confessor, led an army into Scotland, encountered Macbeth near Dunsinane, defeated him, and left Malcolm in possession. Macbet
2 CONC h retired into the North, and the contest was only ended in 1056, by his defeat and death at Lumphanan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Malcolm remained at peace with England during the reign of Edward the Confessor, but on the accession of Harold he favoured the attempt of Tostig. After the battle of Hastings he welcomed to his court Edgar the Atheling, with his 
2 CONC mother and two sisters, and soon married one of them, the Princess Margaret. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1070 he invaded England, ravaged Durham, and carried off so many prisoners that for years after English slaves were found in every hamlet of Scotland. This raid was avenged by a more savage and destructive devastation of Northu
2 CONC mbria by William the Conqueror. Malcolm agreed to do homage, and Edgar left his court, but he continued to give his protection to the English exiles. Disputes arose with William Rufus, and in 1091 Malcolm again invaded England, bu
2 CONC t retired without fighting. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William invaded Scotland the next year, but peace was made by the mediation of Duke Robert and Edgar. In 1093 Malcolm once more made an incursion into England and besieged Alnwick Castle. He was attacked by Roger de Mowbray and ki
2 CONC lled in the battle, November 13th of that year. His queen, Margaret, heard the tidings, and died three days later. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Malcolm III Caennmor of Scotland, King of Scotland Born: ABT 1031 Acceded: 25 APR 1058, Scone Abbey, Perthshire Died: 13 NOV 1093, Alnwick Castle Interred: Escorial, Madrid, Spain. Some say died 1098. Slew Macbeth 1057. King of St
2 CONC rathclyde. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Malcolm III (nickname Malcolm Canmore ("Big Head') DT1a c.)1031-1093 
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Scots (1058--93), the son of Duncan I, who was slain by Macbeth in 1040. He returned from exile in 1054, and conquered S Scotland; but he did not become king until he had defeated and killed Macbeth (1057) and disposed of 
2 CONC Macbeth's stepson, Lulach (1058). He married as his second wife the English Princess Margaret (later St Margaret), sister of Edgar the Ætheling, and launched five invasions of England between 1061 and his death in a skirmish near 
2 CONC Alnwick, Northumberland." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward, Prince of SCOTLAND b. ABT 1068 Scotland, d. 16 NOV 1093 Edwards Isle, SCT Edmund, Prince of SCOTLAND b. ABT 1070 Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ethelred, Prince of SCOTLAND b. ABT 1072 Scotland, d. 13 NOV 1093 Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of SCOTLAND, b. ABT 1074 Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edgar, King of Scotland b. ABT 1074/75 Scotland, d. 8 JAN 1106/7 Edinburgh Castle, Edinburgh, Mid-Lothian, Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of SCOTLAND, b. ABT 1076 Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander I, King of SCOTLAND b. ABT 1077/78 Scotland. d. 23 APR 1124 Stirling, Stirlingshire, Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda "Atheling", Princess of Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT David I "the Saint", King of SCOTLAND 
2 CONT
