1 NOTE The eldest son of Duncan I and Sibylla of Northumbria, who was born about 1031.  On his father's accession to the Scottish throne in 1034 he was made King of Strathclyde, although no more than three years old.  He was forced into 
2 CONC exile in England after his father's murder by Macbeth in 1040, but after he grew up raised an army and, with the help of Siward, Earl of Northumberland, conquered Lothian in 1054.  He defeated and killed Macbeth at Lumphanan in Au
2 CONC gust 1057 and Lulach at Essie in March 1058, thereby becoming the unrivalled King of Scots.  He was crowned at Scone on 25 April 1058.  It was probably in the following year that he married Ingibiorg, widow of Thorfinn II, Earl of
2 CONC  Caithness and Jarl or Orkney, and daughter of Finn Arnesson of Brjar, Jarl of Halland, and his wife Bergljot, daughter of Halfdan Sigurdsson, half-brother of Olav II 'the Saint', King of Norway.  She bore him three sons, Duncan I
2 CONC I, Malcolm and Donald, and died before 1069, when Malcolm was married at Dunfermline to his second wife, St Margaret, by whom he had six other sons and two daughters.
2 CONT Malcolm did homage to William I the Conqueror of England in 1072 and to William II in 1091.  In 1093 he invaded Northumberland and was treacherously killed at Alnwick on 13 November 1093.   He was buried first at Tynemouth, but hi
2 CONC s body was later removed to Dunfermline by his son Alexander I.
1 NOTE Malcolm III "Ceanmor (Longneck)" King of Scotland, founder of the dynastythat consolidated royal power in the Scottish kingdom. The son of KingDuncan I, Malcolm lived in exile in England during part of the reign ofhis father's mur
2 CONC derer, Macbeth.  Malcolm killed Macbeth in battle in 1057and then ascended the throne. After the conquest of England by Williamthe Conqueror, in 1066, Malcolm gave refuge to the Anglo-Saxon princeEdgar the Aetheling and his sister
2 CONC s, one of whom, Margaret (later St.Margaret), became his second wife. Malcolm acknowledged the overlordshipof William in 1072 but nevertheless soon violated his feudal obligationsand made five raids into England. During the last o
2 CONC f these invasions hewas killed by the forces of King William II Rufus (reigned 1087-1100),near Alnwick, Northumberland, England. Except for a brief interval afterMalcolm's death, the Scottish throne remained in his family until th
2 CONC edeath of Queen Margaret, the Maid of Norway, in 1290. Of Malcolm's sixsons by Margaret, three succeeded to the throne: Edgar (reigned1097-1107), Alexander I (1107-24), and David I (1124-53).
