1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''d7 Jacobus''' * c. 1735 ≈ Drakenstein (Paarl) 23.10.1735,burger v. Swellendam x Stellenbosch 25.2.1770 Elizabeth DE JAGER ≈ Drakenstein (Paarl) 10.11.1743 d.v. Andries Pieter de Jager en Elizabeth Potgieter
2 CONT
2 CONT '''e7 Andries Petrus''' * c. 1781 ≈ 15.4.1781 † Riversdal - 22.8.1853 x Caledon 9.10.1814 Maria Magdalena DE JAGER * c. 1793 ≈ 29.7.1793 † Riversdal 4.1.1860 d.v. Carel Pieter de Jager en Johanna Margaretha Blom
2 CONT
2 CONT Cape Civil Deaths - Entry : 2155 Filed 17 September 1853 - Andries Petrus van Wyk passed away on 22 August 1853 at his residence on the farm ' Jongensfontein ; Riversdale District. He was aged 72 years and 9 months, and an Agricul
2 CONC turist and was married. Left movable property. Death Notice signed by : J.J. van Wyk  - Son
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notas deur David Abraham Swanepoel'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Die volgende uittreksel uit die gepubliseerde  dagboek  van die sendeling Backhouse verwys waarskynlik na die persoon.   Ek interpreteer  sy stefplek op sy sterfkennis ook as Jonkersfontein en  nie Jongensfontein  nie.  Daar is we
2 CONC liswaar ‘n Jongensfontein aan die kus naby Riversdal. Die jaar is 1839.
2 CONT
2 CONT 27th. We recrossed the Langeberg at the Platte Kloof and reached our wagon at Jonkers Fontein in the afternoon.  Andries P. Van Wyk being one of the elders of the Zwellendam Church, who hold meetings in their own houses, and he  h
2 CONC aving extended a special invitation among his neighbours, in consequence of our expecting to be with them on the  morrow, several of them had already come to his house. We  were invited to sup with them, and way was made for a rel
2 CONC igious opportunity of an explanatory and devotional character,  in which a Scotch emigrant, connected with the family, interpreted. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 28th. We assembled at half-past eight, and again at two  o'clock, with the family of A. P. Van Wyk and a considerable  number of other persons, chiefly their family connexions, several  of whom were pious. Much openness for religi
2 CONC ous labour was felt, and a precious sense of the love of God prevailed;  we were enabled to elucidate the doctrine of die immediate teaching of the Holy Spirit, to the edification of some of the company. A Frenchman, who emigrated
2 CONC  to the colony  about thirty years ago, was present, and received gratefully a copy of Barclay's Apology, in his own tongue, which he was delighted to hear spoken, however imperfectly. Some grave old men took off their white caps,
2 CONC  as our host also did his hat, when he asked a blessing before meals, and when some others of the company returned thanks after them. The practice of wearing the hat in the house is not unfrequent among  the descendents of the Dut
2 CONC ch Colonists. The provision of the table was ample ; but nothing like immoderate eating which we had heard charged upon this class of the population, was to be seen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Bron:''
2 CONT
2 CONT J. Backhouse. 1844.  A narrative of a visit to the Mauritius and South Africa. London: Hamilton, Adams & Co.  p117.  
2 CONT
