1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DEATH: https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-CSKD-F3G4?cat=993684
2 CONT *Birth Date  c. 10/31/1683  
2 CONT *Death Date  1/18/1750  
2 CONT *First Name  Isabeau  
2 CONT *Last Name  du Preez  
2 CONT *Maiden Name  Prevost
2 CONT *Gender  Female  
2 CONT *Birth Location  Calais, Aquitaine, Dordogne, France  
2 CONT *Baptism Date  10/31/1683  
2 CONT *Death Location  Waveren, Tulbagh, Cape, South Africa -- no Tulbagh existed in 1750 !
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.ballfamilyrecords.co.uk/burger/I224.html#%23I109 Richard Ball] - notes
2 CONT
2 CONT "Botha, C.G. - The French Refugees at the Cape page 81 Elizabeth Pruvost, born at Marcq, November, 1683.
2 CONT
2 CONT Boucher, M -The Cape Huguenots from the Calaisis, in Familia page 15 Elizabeth was born, not in Marck itself but ... but at Les Attaques, south east of Calais, on the road to Ardres. The date should read October 31, 1678, not Nove
2 CONC mber. (The source for this statement is given as the Guines transcript)"
2 CONT
2 CONT SAG Volume8 Pi-Q pp 449 lists her as b5 CJB
2 CONT
2 CONT Geslagsregisters van ou Kaapse Families lists her as b4 CJB
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ::* Charles Prevost ::* Abraham Prevost - 17 ::* Anne Prevost - 6 ::* '''Elisabeth Prevost - 5''' ::* Jacob Prevost (born at Table Bay) ::: Source: Coertzen, Pieter - "The Huguenots of South Africa 1688-1988", Tafelberg Publishers
2 CONC  Limited, Cape Town, 1988 (Contributor June 19,1999 Sonia Tkachuk van Heerden)<ref>Source: [http://www.olivetreegenealogy.com/ships/sahug07.shtml Olivetreegenealogy Ships Carrying Huguenots to South Africa Page 7], seen July 19, 2
2 CONC 014 by Van der Walt-440| Philip van der Walt.</ref> ::  ::: '''Elsewhere in the [http://www.olivetreegenealogy.com/ships/sahug14.shtml same source]: :
2 CONT ::* Charles Prevost (1650-1688) and Marie le Fèvre (1651-1701) ::* Abraham Prevost (1679-1767) ::* Anne Prevost (1681-aft.1725) ::* '''Isabeau Prevost (1683-1750)''' ::: Sources: mostly Appendix 2 of "Hugenotebloed in ons are" by 
2 CONC J.G. leRoux (1992; ISBN 0-7969-0566-5) and "French speakers at the Cape" by M. Boucher (1981, ISBN 0-86981-222-X) (Contributor Lesley Robertson)<ref>Source: [http://www.olivetreegenealogy.com/ships/sahug14.shtml Olivetreegenealogy
2 CONC  Ships Carrying Huguenots to South Africa page 14], seenJuly 19, 2014 by Van der Walt-440 | Philip van der Walt.</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT === Events ===::  Sy het 'n paar keer namens ''Philippe du Pre'' kontrakte in die Kaap gaan onderteken. Sy is 'n tweede keer getroud met ''Christian Gorbrechts'', *1686 + c 10/1731, hy is die wewenaar van ''Sara Gous''. Christian 
2 CONC Gorbrechts is afkomstig van Magdeburg, Duitsland. Hy is 'n soldaat in diens van die VOC van 1710 tot 1714. Christian en Isabeau laat 'n testament opstel op 8 Julie 1723 in Stellenbosch. Philippe se weduwee verloor die plaas ''De S
2 CONC oete Inval'' as gevolg van haar tweede huwelik wat in 'n egskeiding eindig. Isaac de Villiers het die plaas ''De Soete Inval'', Paarl, op 9 Julie 1728 op die vendusie van ''Elisabeth Prevost'' gekoop. Sy moes self dan ook die twee
2 CONC  plase ''De Hoop'' en ''Artois'' terugkoop van haar geskeide man. Sy het daarna self op ''Artois'' gaan boer. <ref name="text56">May  5, 2013 by Vermaak-85 | DinaVermaak.</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT Death Date 1/18/1750
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Botha, C.G. - The French Refugees at the Cape page 81 Elizabeth Pruvost, born at Marcq, November, 1683.
2 CONT
2 CONT Boucher, M -The Cape Huguenots from the Calaisis, in Familia page 15 Elizabeth was born, not in Marck itself but ... but at Les Attaques, south east of Calais, on the road to Ardres. The date should read October 31, 1678, not Nove
2 CONC mber. (The source for this statement is given as the Guines transcript)"
2 CONT
2 CONT SAG Volume8 Pi-Q pp 449 lists her as b5 CJB
2 CONT
2 CONT Geslagsregisters van ou Kaapse Families lists her as b4 CJB
2 CONT
2 CONT Dogter van: Charles Prévot
2 CONT
2 CONT Prevost in Boucher
2 CONT Charles Prevost was born about the year 1650 to Henri Prevost and Jeanne de Fief. He came from a village described in the Guines registers as Dombroy, probably Dombrie, near Saint-Amand-les-Eaux, south-east of Lille towards Valenc
2 CONC iennes. In 1673 he was working in Dunkirk as a master wheelwright and on October 8 of that year married Marie le Fevre, a year his junior and the daughter of David le Fevre and Elisabeth le Bleu of Marck. As all their children fro
2 CONC m France were born near Calais, it seems likely that their father left Dunkirk soon after his marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Prevosts were accompanied on the voyage to the Cape by a son Abraham, who celebrated his ninth birthday at sea, and by two younger daughters, Anne and Elisabeth. A second son Jacob was born on shipboard on May 29, 1688. Abraha
2 CONC m was born at Marck on May 24, 1679, Anne in the same village on February 18, 1681 and Elisabeth. in the hamlet of Les Attaques, south of Marck, on October 31, 1683. Two other children died before the departure for the Cape: David
2 CONC , born at Marck on March 10, 1675, and Marie, born at lower Marck on February 10, 1678 and baptized in Calais a week later. Marie’s death is not recorded in the Guines registers, but David died at Marck on February 9, 1685, shortl
2 CONC y before his tenth birthday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Charles Prevost came from the Lille region, it would seem that the family was originally from the Calaisis, perhaps from Hames- Boucres, north-west of Guines. There were certainly branches there and in Calais itself. ..
2 CONT
2 CONT Boucher.M (1981). French speakers at the Cape: The European Background. Pretoria, UNISA. CHAPTER NINE Cape settlers V: from Flanders to Alsace on the turbulent frontier pp253-4
2 CONT Biography
