1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A woman with a fascinating background. Known as "Van Wijk," her genetic father had been Mijndert Claasen, and her stepfather was her mother's second husband, Roelof van Wijk. Her mother, Dorothea Esterhuijzen, was 
2 CONC the premarital lovechild of Johanna Catharina Campher (later married to Willem van Wijk) and Andries Esterhuizen, who both went on to have large families with other partners. Through Claasen, Elsje is descended from three importan
2 CONC t enslaved progenetrixes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Ansela-van-de-Caap-SM-PROG/6000000002486633225?through=4034167148560076001] was a half-slave born at Cape Town in the late 1660s, whose enslaved mother had arrived at the Cape aboard the Hasselt in 155
2 CONC 8 from Grand Popo on the Gulf of Benin. Ansela married Lourens Campher, and they farmed at what was then De Drie Sprong, in Stellenbosch, today known as Muratie, which uses to promote the estate their romantic story, in which a gr
2 CONC acious benefactor aided Ansela's transition from slavery to manumission, along with the several children Ansela and Lourens had already had. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1676, the progenitor Cornelis "Kees de Boer" Claasen, originally from Utrecht, had married Catharina (Catrijn) van Malabar, another genealogically important progenitrix of Indian origin, who had arrived enslaved at the Cape in 
2 CONC 1657. Their granddaughter, [https://www.geni.com/people/Johanna-Campher-b1c1/4306623459850028100,] married  [https://www.geni.com/people/Jan-Andries-Esterhuysen-b3/6000000001874386025], a grandson of Catharina Wagenmakers van de C
2 CONC aep & Andries Beijers. Catharina "van de Caep", born a half-slave, was baptized on 9 September 1662; it is unclear who her enslaved mother or her European father were, but she became known as "Wagenmakers" after marrying Beijers, 
2 CONC who was the colony's wagon maker. Both Catharina and Andries died in 1700 on their farm at Bottelary, then called "Weltevreden" but known at "Hartenberg" since the early 1700s, when ownership passed to their daughter Elizabeth and
2 CONC  her husband, Christoffel Esterhuijzen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Within this group of the ancestors of Elsje van Wyk, Johanna Catharina Campher went on to marry and have several children with Willem Willemsz van Wijk, and her sister Sophia married Christoffel van Wijk—both therefore becoming im
2 CONC portant "Stammoders" of the van Wyks of South Africa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT All of these families were clearly closely connected. 
