2 CONC ingla, from c. 1230. That saga cannot be taken as an accurate source for Olaf's life, but most of the following description is based on the narrative there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Norway during the reign of St. Olaf (1015–1028) showing areas under the control of hereditary chieftains (petty kingdoms). After some years' absence in England, fighting the Danes, he returned to Norway in 1015 and declared himsel
2 CONC f king, obtaining the support of the five petty kings of the Uplands. In 1016 he defeated Earl Sweyn, hitherto the virtual ruler of Norway, at the Battle of Nesjar. He founded the town Borg by the waterfall Sarpr, later to be know
2 CONC n as Sarpsborg. Within a few years he had won more power than had been enjoyed by any of his predecessors on the throne.
2 CONT
