2 CONT 3.         ROBERT (-Nikaia 22 Jul 1035, bur Nikaia basilica St Mary, transferred [1187] to Apulia).  Guillaume de Jumièges names (in order) "Richard, Robert et Guillaume" as the three sons of Duke Richard II and Judith[190].  Adem
2 CONC ar names Robert as brother of Richard[191].  Guillaume de Jumièges records that he rebelled against his brother Duke Richard III from his stronghold at Falaise[192].  He succeeded his brother in 1027 as ROBERT II "le Diable" Duke 
2 CONC of Normandy.  "Rotbertus Normannorum dux, Ricardi filio" founded the abbey of Sainte-Trinité at Rouen in 1030[193].  He gave shelter to Henri, son of Robert II King of France, during his dispute with his mother Queen Constance, th
2 CONC e king granting le Vexin to Robert after his accession to the French throne in 1031[194].  William of Malmesbury records that Robert went on pilgrimage to Jerusalem in 1035[195].  Orderic Vitalis dates his departure to "after seve
2 CONC n and a half years", but it is unclear from the context whether this is calculated based on his accession or his father's death[196].  The date of Robert´s departure can be estimated more precisely from his charter dated 13 Jan 10
2 CONC 35 which announces his forthcoming departure for Jerusalem[197].  Guillaume de Jumièges records the death of Duke Robert 2 Jul 1035 at Nikea on his return from a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, and his burial in the basilica of St Mary a
2 CONC t Nikaia[198].  According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, he died while on pilgrimage in 1031[199].  Orderic Vitalis specifies that Duke Robert died "in the city of Nicæa in Bythinia"[200].  The Brevis Relatio de Origine Willelmi Co
2 CONC nquestoris records that "Robertus…Ricardi filius" was buried "rediens de Hierusalem…in Nica civitate"[201].  William of Malmesbury recounts that his remains were disinterred from Nikaia on the orders of his son, but interred in Ap
2 CONC ulia on their way back to France after the messenger learnt of the death of William I King of England[202].   Mistress (1): ---.  Robert de Torigny names "Aeliz" as daughter of Duke Robert II "de alia concubina" from Herleve[203].
2 CONC   The name of Duke Robert's first mistress is not known.   Mistress (2): HERLEVE [Arlette], daughter of FULBERT [de Falaise] & his wife Doda [Duwa] ---.  Guillaume de Jumièges names "Herlève fille le Fulbert valet de chamber du du
2 CONC c" as mother of Duke Guillaume II, recording that "un certain Herluin, brave chevalier, prit Herlève pour femme" after the death of Duke Robert[204].   Orderic Vitalis calls her "Duke Robert's concubine", and records her marriage,
2 CONC  referring to her husband as stepfather to Duke Guillaume[205].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines refers to the mother of Duke Guillaume as "filia…Herbertus pelliparius et uxor eius Doda sive Duwa", specifying that they
2 CONC  were from Chaumont in the diocese of Liège but moved to Falaise but that others said they were from Huy, and refers to her marriage to "Herlewino de Vado comitis"[206].  She married Herluin de Conteville.  
