2 CONT ◦Somerled var Lord av Cantyre og Hebridene. Senere ble han konge over Hebridene
2 CONT  st Norway, Medieval Scandinavia and Ancient Genealogy 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 'Somerled' means "summer wanderer", the name given to the Vikings). He took the title Rí Innse Gall (King of the Hebrides) as well as King of Man.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Somerled's death in 1164 three of his sons divided his kingdom between them:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Aonghus (ancestor of the McRuari or McRory)
2 CONT     Dughall (ancestor of Clan MacDougall)
2 CONT     Ragnald, whose son Donald Mor McRanald would give his name to the Clan Donald which would contest territory with the MacDougalls.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Haakon IV of Norway (reigned 1217–1263) confirmed Donald's son Angus Mor (the Elder) Mac Donald (the first Macdonald) as Lord of Islay, and the two participated jointly in the Battle of Largs (1263). When that ended with an e
2 CONC ffective victory for Scotland, Angus Mor accepted King Alexander III of Scotland as his (nominal) overlord and retained his own territory. Council of the Isles The ruins of Finlaggan Castle on Eilean Mòr, Loch Finlaggan, on the is
2 CONC land of Islay, where the Council of the Isles met.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Lord was advised (at least on an occasional basis) by a Council. Dean Monro of the Isles, who wrote a description of the Western Isles in 1549, described the membership as consisting of four ranks:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Four "great men of the royal blood of Clan Donald lineally descended" (Macdonald of Clanranald, Macdonald of Dunnyvaig, MacIain of Ardnamurchan and Macdonald of Keppoch)
2 CONT     Four "greatest of the nobles, called lords" (Maclean of Duart, Maclaine of Lochbuie, Macleod of Dunvegan and Macleod of the Lewes)
2 CONT     Four "thanes of less living and estate" (Mackinnon of Strath, MacNeil of Barra, MacNeill of Gigha and Macquarrie of Ulva)
2 CONT     "Freeholders or men that had their lands in factory" (Mackay of the Rhinns, MacNicol of Scorrybreac, MacEacharn of Kilellan, Mackay of Ugadale, Macgillivray in Mull and Macmillan of Knapdale).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In practice, membership and attendance must have varied with the times and the occasion. A commission granted in July 1545 by Domhnall Dubh, claimant to the Lordship, identified the following members:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hector Maclean of Duart
2 CONT     John Macdonald of Clanranald
2 CONT     Ruari Macleod of the Lewes
2 CONT     Alexander Macleod of Dunvegan
2 CONT     Murdoch Maclaine of Lochbuie
2 CONT     Allan Maclean of Torloisk
2 CONT     Archibald Macdonald, Captain of Clann Uisdein
2 CONT     Alexander MacIan of Ardnamurchan
2 CONT     John Maclean of Coll
2 CONT     Gilleonan MacNeil of Barra
2 CONT     Ewen Mackinnon of Strath
2 CONT     John MacQuarrie of Ulva
2 CONT     John Maclean of Ardgour
2 CONT     Alexander Macdonell of Glengarry
2 CONT     Angus Macdonald of Knoydart
2 CONT     Donald Maclean of Kingairloch
2 CONT     Angus Macdonald, brother of James Macdonald of Dunnyveg.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lords of the Isles
2 CONT
2 CONT Angus Òg (Angus the Young), Angus Mòr's (Angus the Great) younger son (or grandson), gave assistance to Robert the Bruce at the Battle of Bannockburn in 1314, and in reward kept control of the Isles and gained most of the land con
2 CONC fiscated from the McDougalls for backing the defeated side. Angus Og's son Good John of Islay first formally assumed the title Dominus Insularum – Lord of the Isles – in 1336.
2 CONT
2 CONT In their maritime domain the Lords of the Isles used galleys for both warfare and transport. These ships had developed from the Viking longships and knarrs, clinker-built with a square sail and rows of oars. From the 14th century 
2 CONC they changed from using a steering oar to a stern rudder. These ships took part in sea battles and attacked castles or forts built close to the sea. The Lordship specified the feudal dues of its subjects in terms of numbers and si
2 CONC zes of the galleys each area had to provide in service to their Lord. Cara from the air
2 CONT
2 CONT Successive Lords of the Isles fiercely asserted their independence, culminating in 1462 with John MacDonald II of the Isles making a treaty with Edward IV of England to conquer Scotland with him and the Earl of Douglas. The civil 
2 CONC war in England, known famously as the Wars of the Roses, prevented the activation of this alliance and on the discovery of his treason in 1493 John Macdonald II forfeited his estates and titles to James IV of Scotland. Since then,
2 CONC  the eldest male child of the reigning Scottish (and later, British) monarch has been styled "Lord of the Isles". The office itself has been extinct since the 15th century and the style since then has no other meaning but to recal
2 CONC l the Scottish destruction of the ancient Norse-Gaelic lordship (and indeed the self-destruction of the MacDonalds).
2 CONT
2 CONT Currently Charles, Prince of Wales is styled Lord of the Isles. Tiny Cara off Kintyre, which is owned by the MacDonalds of Largie, is reputedly the only island still in the possession of direct descendants of the Lords of the Isle
2 CONC s.[4] See also
2 CONT
