2 CONT 1  UID B23373A31F86304EAA5A005624405B32692E
1 NOTE He was a Yeoman, ruling in West Argyll.
1 NOTE He was a Yeoman, ruling in West Argyll.
1 NOTE NEWLIN LINE - 23rd ggrandfather 
2 CONT
2 CONT !Galloway, covering most of Ayrshire, was inhabited by a mixed race, partly Scots settlers from Ireland of a different stock from that of the Dalriads or Irish Scots of Argyleshire, partly Picts who had acquired possessions among 
2 CONC them.  Hence the Galwegians are sometimes called the wild Scots of Galloway. [WBH - Scotland]
2 CONT
2 CONT !Am guessing this is Somerled, head of the sept of the MacDonalds, the most powerful scion of those original Scots who colonized Argyleshire under Fergus. This needs verification. [WBH - Scotland]
2 CONT
2 CONT !Appears to have been ceded the Sudreyjar or Southern Isles by his brother-in-law, Godred, King of Man. [The Bruces and the Cumyns, p. 497]
2 CONT
2 CONT The clan Macdougall takes its name from Dugall, son of Somerled, King of the Isled, who, after his father's death in 1164, held most of Argyll together with the islands of Mull, Lismore, Jura, Tiree, Coll and many others. [Clans a
2 CONC nd Tartans, p. 178]
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Gillebride; m. Ragnhildis; father of Reginald who m. Fonia of Moray. [WFT Vol 2 Ped 2431]
2 CONT
2 CONT On King David I's death in 1153, Somerled, Lord of Argyll, took advantage of the weak situation of his successor, 11-year-old Malcolm IV the Maiden, and sailed up the Clyde and sacked Glasgow. [Scotland: A Concise History, p. 31]
2 CONT
2 CONT Somerled (c1126-64) was a pivotal figure in west Highland history at a period when a more homogenous Hebridean society was forming out of its Norse and Celtic parts. Though a flimsy Norse influence was to linger for another centur
2 CONC y, that culture declined as the language and culture of Gaeldom flourished. According to Clan Donald historians, Somerled's mother was Norse while his father, Gillebride, claimed descent from the kings of Dalriada. His name, Sumar
2 CONC -lidi (Summer Traveller, the season when Vikings went looting) is gaelicised as Somhairle and anglicised as Sorley.
2 CONT    Somerled waged war against Norse settlers in the southern Hebrides until in 1156, with a fleet said to number 80 galleys, he won a great victory off Islay. He claimed and married Ragnhilde, daughter of Olaf, King of Man, and ac
2 CONC quired the title 'Regulus' (sub-king) of Argyll, rendered in Gaelic Ri Innse Gall is Ceann Tir, Ruler of the Strangers' Isles and Kintyre. His main fortress was probably Dunivaig, Islay; Claig Castle off Jura may also have associa
2 CONC tions. Remarkably, considering the lack of detailed information from this time, Somerled is also credited with the invention or introduction of the hinged rudder, which might well have contributed to his success over conventional 
2 CONC longships. A grave-slab said to carry his effigy is to be seen at Saddell Abbey in Kintyre, founded from Mellifont in Ireland in 1160, no doubt under his patronage. [Collins Encyclopaedia of Scotland, pp. 889-90]
2 CONT
2 CONT From the time of Somerled in the middle of the 12th century until their final forfeiture in 1493, the Lords of the Isles controlled large parts of the Western Isles and western seaboard of Scotland. Their influence within their ow
2 CONC n territories was enormous, and hopes for a resurgence of their power were not finally abandoned by the island clans until the final defeat at Culloden in 1746.
2 CONT    Somerled claimed descent through his father Gillebride from the kings of Dalriada, and styled himself Ri Innse Gall is Cind itre -- Ruler of the Islands of the Strangers (Hebrides) and Kintyre. In Latin he was called Rex Insula
2 CONC rum--King of the Isles. He had a son Reginald/Rognvaldr, whose son Donald gave his name to the Clan Donald. [Collins Encyclopaedia of Scotland, p. 548]
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Gillebride, Prince of Argyll, and Agatha Hakonsdottir. [Larry Overmire <larryover@worldnet.att.net, 2 Apr 2003]
2 CONT
2 CONT Somerled, King of Argyll; married Raghnild, daughter of Olaf, King of Manand the Isles (a realm comprising the Iom, Hebrides, and western islandsoff Scotland that flourished from the late 11th centruy to 1266), and waskilled at Re
2 CONC nfrew 1164 campaigning against Malcolm IV King of Scots.[Burke's Peerage]
2 CONT
