1 OCCU Lord of Bute & Arran
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Angus, Lord of Bute and Arran''' was the son of Somerled, Thane of Argyll and Ragnhild. He inherited Bute, Arran and Gamoran, but lost these to his brother Ranald. He was killed, along with his three sons, by th
2 CONC e men of Skye. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Angus, Lord of Bute and Arran
2 CONT
2 CONT * James Macrory, Lord of Bute, d. 1210
2 CONT * son
2 CONT * son
2 CONT
2 CONT "The surviving sons of Ragnhilda -- Ranald, Dugall, and Angus -- divided the bulk of Somerled's possessions between themselves. Angus, the younger, received a scattered patrimony comprising the 'rough bounds' of Garmoran -- Moidar
2 CONC t, Morar, Knoydart, and Arisaig, to the north of Ardnamurchan, and also the islands of Arran and Bute. In 1192 he quarrelled with Ranald and defeated him in a battle, but in 1210 he was himself killed together with his three sons 
2 CONC by the Norsemen of Skye who had invaded Moidart. His possessions were seized by Ranald's descendants, although his eldest son James had left an heiress Jane [Jean], who married Alexander, son of Walter Fitz-Alan the Steward. Throu
2 CONC gh her the Stewarts prosecuted a claim to Arran and Bute -- which they eventually obtained after some resistance." (Williams, 127)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT *Engus, Angus, or Aonghus (ancestor of the McRuari or McRory) 
2 CONT
2 CONT c) ENGUS (-killed 1210). The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names “Dulgallum, Reginaldum, Engus et Olavum” as the four sons of “Sumerledo regulo Herergaildel” and his wife the daughter of Olav King of Man[356]. The Chronicon Manniæ
2 CONC  et Insularum records that Engus, son of Somerled, defeated his brother Reginald in 1192, adding in a later passage that Engus was killed in 1210[357].
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Charles Mosley, editor, ''Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage'', 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 449. Hereinafter cited as Burke's Peera
2 CONC ge and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://www.isle-of-man.com/manxnotebook/manxsoc/msvol22/p074.htm Chronicon Manniae et Insularum] (Chronicle of Man and the Isles) - 1164-1225.* Ronald Williams, ''The Lords of the Isles'' (1984)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Wikipedia: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aonghas_mac_Somhairle Aonghas mac Somhairle]
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT
2 CONT *1.         [https://www.geni.com/people/Somerled-King-of-the-Isles/6000000002043212109 SOMERLED] (-killed in battle 1164).    m  [https://www.geni.com/people/Ragnhildr-%C3%93l%C3%A1fsd%C3%B3ttir-of-Man/6000000019032360275?through
2 CONC =6000000002043212109 --- of Man], illegitimate daughter of OLAV King of Man & his mistress ---.  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that “Olavus filius Godredi Crovan” had many concubines by whom he fathered “filios tres…Re
2 CONC ignaldum, Lagmannum et Haraldum et filias multas”, adding that one daughter married “Sumerledo regulo Herergaildel”[1024].  Balfour Paul names her "Ragnhildis" but he does not cite the corresponding primary source[1025].  Somerled
2 CONC  & his wife had five children:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT >*c)         '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Angus-of-Bute-Arran/6000000003078496086?through=6000000002043212109 ANGUS] (-killed 1210).  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum names “Dulgallum, Reginaldum, Engus et Olavum” as the four 
2 CONC sons of “Sumerledo regulo Herergaildel” and his wife the daughter of Olav King of Man[1029].  The Chronicon Manniæ et Insularum records that Engus, son of Somerled, defeated his brother Reginald in 1192, adding in a later passage 
2 CONC that Engus was killed in 1210[1030].  Balfour Paul states that Angus inherited "Bute, with a part of Arran, and the Rough Bounds (Garmoran) extending from Ardnamurchan to Gleneig"[1031].  m ---.  The name of Angus’s wife is not kn
2 CONC own.  Angus & his wife had one child:'''
2 CONT >>* i)          [https://www.geni.com/people/James-Macrory-Lord-of-Bute/6000000004831216872?through=6000000003078496086 JAMES] .  Balfour Paul names James as son of Angus, but does not cite the corresponding primary source[1032]. 
2 CONC  Lord of Bute and Arran.  m ---.  The name of James’s wife is not known.  James & his wife had [one child]: 
2 CONT >>>*(a)       [https://www.geni.com/people/Jean-Macrory/6000000021718774080?through=6000000004831216872 JEAN] .  Balfour Paul names Jean as daughter of James Lord of Bute and Arran, and records her marriage to Alexander Stewart, b
2 CONC ut does not cite the corresponding primary source[1033].  Andrew McEwen suggests that there is no evidence to indicate that the wife of Alexander Stewart was the daughter of James Lord of Bute[1034].  Heiress of the isles of Bute 
2 CONC and Arran.  m ALEXANDER Stewart High Steward of Scotland, son of WALTER FitzAlan High Steward of Scotland & his wife Beatrice of Angus (-1283).] 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#_ftnref1019
