0 @N6000000002187723658@ NOTE Welf II, Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Welf II (1072 – 24 September 1120, Kaufering), or Welfhard, called Welf the Fat, was duke of Bavaria from 1101 until his death. In the Welf genealogy, he is counted as Welf V.
1 CONT
1 CONT Welf was the oldest son of Welf I, Duke of Bavaria, and his wife Judith of Flanders. In 1089, he was married to Matilda of Tuscany, who was 26 years older, in order to strengthen the relation between his family and the pope during
1 CONC  the Investiture Controversy between king and pope. During King Henry IV's Italian campaign of 1090, Welf and Matilda fought against the King.
1 CONT
1 CONT Since Matilda had secretly transferred her property to the Church before her marriage, Welf left her in 1095 and, together with his father, changed sides to King Henry IV, possibly in exchange for a promise of succeeding his fathe
1 CONC r as duke of Bavaria.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his father's death in 1101 Welf indeed inherited the office of duke of Bavaria. He continued his alliance with the kings; he did not remarry and died childless in 1120. Welf was buried in the Abbey of Weingarten.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Welf I Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT 1101-1120 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry IX 
0 @N6000000000437175157@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Emperor Name Suffix:<NSFX> III, of the HRE "The Black"
0 @N6000000002187690044@ NOTE Henry IX, Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry IX (died 13 December 1126), called the Black(Schulzbacher), a member of the House of Welf, was duke of Bavaria from 1120 to 1126.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was the second son of Welf I, Duke of Bavaria. As a young man, he administered the family's property south of the Alps. Through his marriage to Wulfhild, daughter of Magnus, Duke of Saxony, he acquired part of the Billung pr
1 CONC operty in Saxony.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1116, he joined Emperor Henry V's Italian campaign. He succeeded his brother Welf II, Duke of Bavaria, when the latter died childless in 1120.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the royal election of 1125, he supported his son-in-law Frederick II, Duke of Swabia, but switched his allegiance to Lothair, Duke of Saxony, after Lothair promised that Gertrud, his only daughter and heir, would marry Henry's 
1 CONC son Henry.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Lothair won the election and banned Frederick, in 1126 Henry abdicated as duke of Bavaria and retired to the family foundation of Weingarten Abbey so that he did not have to take part in the prosecution of his son-in-law. He
1 CONC nry died shortly thereafter and was buried in Weingarten.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Judith, married Frederick II, Duke of Swabia 
1 CONT Conrad, died 17 March 1126 
1 CONT Henry X the Proud 
1 CONT Welf 
1 CONT Sophia, married Berthold III, Duke of Zähringen and Leopold I, Duke of Styria 
1 CONT Wulfhild, married Rudolf, Count of Bregenz 
1 CONT Mathilde, married Diepold IV, Margrave of Vohburg and Gebhard III, Count of Sulzbach 
1 CONT Adalbert, Abbot of Corvey 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Welf II Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT 1120-1126 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry X 
