0 @N6000000003949917541@ NOTE Note: I (Jim Weber) make Henry the "IX"th Duke of Bavaria (as does the Encyclopaedia Britannica). Turton also names him Henry I, while adding Saxony to his Ducal title; therefore he is probably Henry I Duke of Saxony and IX Duke o
1 CONC f Bavaria. This supposition is indirectly confirmed by the Encyclopaedia Britannica, which names his son Henry X of Bavaria and Henry II of Saxony. (AR is incorrect in naming him Henry I of Bavaria).
0 @N6000000005598776739@ NOTE He suceeded his brother Guelph as duke in 1120.  He was one of the med iators in the settlement of hte long struggle over investitures. Afte r the death of Calixtus II, he supported Frederick of Hohenstauffen fo r Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT (ref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_IX%2C_Duke_of_Bavaria) HenryI X (died 13 December 1126), called the Black(Schulzbacher), a member o f the House of Welf, was duke of Bavaria from 1120 to 1126.
1 CONT Henry was the second son of Welf I, Duke of Bavaria. As a young man, h e administered the family's property south of the Alps. Through his ma rriage to Wulfhild, daughter of Magnus, Duke of Saxony, he acquired pa rt of the Billung
1 CONC  property in Saxony.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1116, he joined Emperor Henry V's Italian campaign. He succeeded hi s brother Welf II, Duke of Bavaria, when the latter died childless in1 120.  In the royal election of 1125, he supported his son-in-law Frede rick II, Duke of 
1 CONC Swabia, but switched his allegiance to Lothair, Duke of Saxony, after Lothair promised that Gertrud, his only daughter and heir, would marry Henry's son Henry.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Lothair won the election and banned Frederick, in 1126 Henry abd icated as duke of Bavaria and retired to the family foundation of Wein garten Abbey so that he did not have to take part in the prosecution o f his son-in-law.
1 CONC  Henry died shortly thereafter and was buried in Wein garten.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry had the following children: 1.) Judith, married Frederick II, Du ke of Swabia; 2.) Conrad, died 17 March 1126; 3.) Henry X the Proud; 4 .) Welf; 5.) Sophia, married Berthold III, Duke of Zähringen and Leopo ld I, Duke of Sty
1 CONC ria; 6.) Wulfhild, married Rudolf, Count of Bregenz;7 .) Mathilde, married Diepold IV, Margrave of Vohburg and Gebhard III, Count of Sulzbach; and 8.) Adalbert, Abbot of Corvey
0 @N6000000000437069215@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Princess Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Normandy
0 @N6000000002187570229@ NOTE Eleanor of Normandy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor was the daughter of Richard II of Normandy and Judith of Brittany, she was married to Baldwin IV of Flanders in 1031. Eleanor was the mother of Judith, the wife of Welf I, Duke of Bavaria. She died in Flanders after 1071.
0 @N6000000003319688039@ NOTE
1 CONT http://www.peterwestern.f9.co.uk/maximilia/pafg81.htm#947
0 @N6000000000269774274@ NOTE Baldwin IV of Flanders (980\endash May 30, 1036), known as the Bearded, was Count of Flanders from 988 until his death. He was the son of Arnulf II of Flanders. His mother was Rozala of Lombardy. He was a seventh-generation descen
1 CONC dant of Charlemagne through his father and an eighth-generation one through his mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] History
1 CONT In contrast to his predecessors Baldwin turned his attention to the east and north, leaving the southern part of his territory in the hands of his vassals the counts of Guînes, Hesdin, and St. Pol.
1 CONT
1 CONT To the north of the county Baldwin was given Zeeland as a fief by the Holy Roman Emperor Henry II, while on the right bank of the Scheldt river he received Valenciennes (1013) and parts of the Cambresis and Hainaut.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the French territories of the count of Flanders, the supremacy of the Baldwini remained unchallenged. They organized a great deal of colonization of marshland along the coastline of Flanders and enlarged the harbour and city of
1 CONC  Brugge.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT Baldwin first married Ogive of Luxembourg, by whom he had a son and heir Baldwin V.
1 CONT
1 CONT He later married Eleanor of Normandy (or perhaps Ainor or Judith), widow of Duke Welf of Bavaria and daughter of Richard II of Normandy, by whom he had at least one daughter Judith. This daughter married Tostig Godwinson, Earl of 
1 CONC Northumberland.[1] These family connections demonstrate the political interests of the Flemish counts, both in the Kingdom of France, England and the Holy Roman Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT His granddaughter, Matilda of Flanders, would go on to marry William the Conqueror, therefore starting the line of Anglo-Norman Kings of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ "Tostig, Earl of Northumbria". Dictionary of National Biography 19: 998\endash 1001.
