0 @N6000000003300424062@ NOTE King Canute made Godwine an Anglo-Saxon Earl of Wessex and Godwine very q uickly rose to be extremely powerful. In 1042 Edward the Confessor marrie d Godwine's daughter Edith and from then more or less ran the country. H e opposed
1 CONC  Edward's Norman advisors. In 1051 the Godwines revolted and Edw ard expelled them from the country. About the same time Edward promised W illiam (the Conqueror) that he could have a claim to the English throne a fter Edward died.
1 CONC  This may have been a ploy to prevent the Godwines claim ing the throne themselves. In 1052 the Godwines, including Harold Godwins on, invaded England and Edward reinstated them. Godwine died in followin g year.
1 CONT 1035 Nov 12 Canute dies
1 CONT King Canute died leaving the rule of the country in dispute betwee n Harthacnut, the son        of Emma, and Harold Harefoot the son of Aelf gifu. The Earls of Northumbria and Mercia       supported Harold's clai m while Earl Godw
1 CONC ine supported Harthacanute's.
1 CONT 1045 Jan Edward marries
1 CONT Edward married Edith, the daughter of Earl Godwine. Godwine was t hen in an extremely          powerful position being related to the Kin g of England.
1 CONT 1052 May Godwines invade England
1 CONT Godwine, the Earl of Wessex, his son Harold and a large fleet sa iled up the Thames to          London and forced Edward to reinstate the m into their previous positions of power.
0 @N6000000004533104476@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Godwin (sometimes Godwine) (c.1001 - April 15, 1053), was one of themost powerful lords in England under the Danish king Canute the Greatand his successors. Canute made him the first Earl of Wessex. Godwinwas the father of Harold 
1 CONC II and of Edith of Wessex, wife of Edward theConfessor.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT Godwin was a seventh generation descendant of King Ethelred of Wessex(c. 837-871). Ethelred was succeeded by his younger brother Alfred theGreat because his own son Aethelhelm of Wessex (c. 870-927) was stilla minor at the time of
1 CONC  his death. Aethelhelm was later granted landsby his uncle but was never able to claim the throne. His daughterElfleda of Wessex (c.890-918) however became Queen consort to KingEdward the Elder (c.871-924).
1 CONT
1 CONT Her brother Aethelfrith of Wessex (c.900-927) lived the life of acommon landlord. His son Eadric of Wessex was granted possession ofWashington in the South Downs of West Sussex. His own son Aethelwerdof Wessex (c.930-998) is known
1 CONC  as "the Historian". Aethelmar Cild(c.960-1015), son of the later, was a benefactor of Eynsham Abbey. Hisson was Wulfnoth Cild (c.983-1015) who was Thegn of Sussex but isotherwise obscure. Godwin was son to Wulfnoth.
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 1014, Godwin was first married to Thyra Sveinsdóttir, daughterof Sweyn I , King of Denmark, Norway and England. Thyra however diedin 1018. Her death apparently did not prevent her brother Canute fromcreating Godwin Earl of 
1 CONC Wessex during the same year.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1019, Godwin was married again to Gytha Thorkelsdóttir,granddaughter of the legendary Viking Styrbjörn Starke andgreat-granddaughter to Harold Bluetooth. The marriage resulted in thebirth of at least twelve children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Driella of Wessex (c. 1019)
1 CONT Edith of Wessex, (c. 1020 - December 19, 1075), queen consort ofEdward the Confessor
1 CONT Sweyn Godwinson, Earl of Mercia (c. 1021 - 1052). At some point hedeclared himself an illegitimate son of Canute the Great but this isconsidered to be a false claim.
1 CONT Harold II of England (c. 1022 - October 14, 1066)
1 CONT Gungilda of Wessex, a nun (c. 1024 - 1080)
1 CONT Ylgiva of Wessex (c. 1025)
1 CONT Tostig Godwinson, Earl of Northumbria (c. 1026 - September 25, 1066).
1 CONT Ydgiva of Wessex (c. 1028)
1 CONT Gyrth Godwinson (c. 1032 - October 14, 1066)
1 CONT Leofwine Godwinson , Earl of Kent (c. 1035 - October 14, 1066)
1 CONT Wulfnoth Godwinson (c. 1036 - 1087)
1 CONT Ylfgar of Wessex (c. 1038)
1 CONT On November 12, 1035, Canute died. His kingdoms were divided amongthree rival rulers. Harold Harefoot, illegitimate son by Aelgifu ofNorthampton, usurped the throne of England. Harthacanute, legitimateson by Emma of Normandy, reig
1 CONC ned in Denmark. Norway rebelled underMagnus the Noble. On 1037, the throne of England was reportedlyclaimed by Alfred of Wessex, son of Emma of Normandy and Ethelred theUnready and half-brother of Harthacanute. Godwin is reported 
1 CONC to haveeither captured Alfred himself or to have deceived him by pretendingto be his ally and then surrendering him to the forces of HaroldHarefoot. Either way Alfred was blinded and soon died.
1 CONT
1 CONT On March 17, 1040, Harold Harefoot died and Godwin supported theaccession of Harthacanute to the throne of England. When Harthacanutehimself died (June 8, 1042), Godwin supported the claim of hishalf-brother Edward the Confessor t
1 CONC o the throne. Edward was anotherson of Emma and Ethelred, having spent most of the previous thirtyyears in Normandy. His reign restored the native royal house of Wessexto the throne of England. Despite his alleged responsibility f
1 CONC or thedeath of Edward's brother Alfred, Godwin secured the marriage of hisdaughter Edith (Eadgyth) to Edward in 1045. Godwin soon became theleader of opposition to growing Norman influence as Edward drewadvisors, nobles and priest
1 CONC s from his former place of refuge.
1 CONT
1 CONT Exiled from the kingdom in September 1051 for refusing to punish thepeople of Dover for a violent clash with the visiting Eustace II,Count of Boulogne, Godwin returned the following year with an armedforce, compelling Edward to re
1 CONC store his earldom.
1 CONT
1 CONT On April 15, 1053, Godwin died. His son Harold succeeded him as Earlof Wessex, an area then covering roughly the southernmost third ofEngland. Harold later succeeded Edward the Confessor and became Kingof England in his own right.
0 @N6000000000944958334@ NOTE Jarl. Levde 1018. Død 15.04.1053.  Jarl av Wessex. Gudine (Godwin) var en anglosaksisk stormann. Han nevnes alt i 1018 som jarl av Wessex under Knud den Store av Danmark. Det kom imidlertid til åpen strid mellom Knud og Gudine i 1
1 CONC 051. Han ble dømt til landflyktighet, men vendte tilbake allerede året etter og tiltvang seg atter sine jarledømmer. Fra Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hardrådes saga: ?75. Edvard Adalrådsson var konge i England etter Hordaknut, sin br
1 CONC or. Han ble kalt Edvard den gode (Edward Confessor, 1042-1066), og det var han også. Mor til kong Edvard var dronning Emma, datter til Rikard Rude-jarl (i Rouen). Hennes bror var Robert jarl, far til Viljalm Bastard (Wilhelm Erobr
1 CONC eren), som da var hertug i Ruda i Normandi. Kong Edvard var gift med dronning Gyda, datter til jarlen Gudine (Godwin) Ulvnadsson. Brødrene til Gyda var Toste jarl - han var eldst -, den andre var Morukåre jarl, den tredjeValtjov j
1 CONC arl, den fjerde Svein jarl, den femte Harald - han var yngst. Han vokste opp i kong Edvards hird og var hans fostersønn; kongen elsket ham overmåte høyt og regnet ham som sin egen sønn, for kongen hadde ikke barn selv.?  Han døde 
1 CONC av slag ved kongens bord i 1053.
0 @N6000000001354632760@ NOTE Minister/close counsellor of King Cnut. Originally of obscure origin,Godwine's ability ha d raised him high in the royal favor he was allied to Cnut by marriage, entrusted by him wit h the earldom of Wessex, and at last
1 CONT made the Viceroy or justiciar of the King in the government of the realm. In the wars of Scan dinavia he had shown courage and skill at thehead of a body of English troops, but his tru e field of action lay at home. Shrewd,eloquen
1 CONC t, an active administrator, Godwine united vigilance, industry, and caution with a singular dexterity in the management of men. [WBH - Engla nd]
1 CONT
1 CONT !Gave his daughter Eadgyth in marriage to King Edward the Confessor. He and his sons, Harol d and Sweyn, were lords of all the land from the Humber to the Severn -- the richest half o f England. His younger sons, Wulnoth, Tostig, 
1 CONC Gurtth and Leofwine, were subsequently advance dto high dignities. [Knight's Popular History of England, Vol. 1, p. 187]
1 CONT
1 CONT !Southern England, 1051. Earl Godwin leads a revolt against Norman influence; it fails, an d he is banished with his family apart from his daughter, Queen Edith.
1 CONT Winchester, 15 April 1053. Godwin, who forced a settlement on Edward last year, dies; his so n Harold becomes Earl of Wessex and main royaladviser. [Chronicle of the Royal Family, p. 26 ]
1 CONT
1 CONT King Edward owed his succession in England largely to the efforts of Godwine, the powerful ea rl of Wessex, and as the price of his supportthe king had been compelled to marry Edith th e earl's daughter. Only6 years before Godwine
1 CONC  had been involved in one of the most spectacular crimes of the age: a murder which most closely affected the new king in England. In 1036 A lfred, Edward's younger brother, had come to England, and while in this country he had be
1 CONC en s eized, blinded, and brutally killed. The crime if not instigated by Godwine was undoubtedlyc onnived at by him, and it would seem that Edward the Confessor always regarded as his brother 's murderer the earl on whom he was so
1 CONC  often forced to be dependent. [The Norman Achievement , p. 30]
1 CONT
1 CONT Launched an open rebellion in 1051 following the appointment of Robert, abbot of Jumieges, t o be bishop of London in 1044, and then promoted to the metroipolitan see of Canterbury in 10 51. Siward and Leofriccame to King Edward's
1 CONC  support forcing Godwine and his sons into exile. [The Norman ..., p. 32]
1 CONT
1 CONT Thegn in Sussex, Ealdorman of Wessex 1018; son of Wulfnoth Cild; m. 1019/20 Githa; father o f Harold Godwinson. [Ancestral Roots, p. 5]
1 CONT
1 CONT The history of Berkeley Manor goes back to the reign of Edward the Confessor (1042-66) when i t was held by Earl Godwin, father of King Harold who fell at the battle of Senlac. Earl Godwi n probably had some small dwelling on the 
1 CONC site of the present castle. After the Norman Conque st, Godwin having been despoiled of his possessions, Berkeley was handed over by William th e Conqueror together with other lands to William FitzOsbern, Earl of Hereford. [The Hi
1 CONC story o f Berkeley Castle
0 @N6000000000945049095@ NOTE Gyda flyktet i 1067 til en klippeøy i Bristolfjorden sammen med flere høvdinger. 
1 CONT Hun måtte så flykte videre til St. Omer i Flandern, hvor hun døde.
0 @N6000000002187757604@ NOTE Gytha Thorkelsdóttir
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gyda Torkelsdotter was the daughter of Torkel Styrbjörnsson. Consequently she was the granddaughter of the disinherited Swedish prince Styrbjörn Starke, the conqueror of Jomsborg, and Tyra, the daughter of Harold Bluetooth king of
1 CONC  Norway and Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1019, she married the Anglo-Saxon nobleman Godwin of Wessex. Among their children were Harold II of England and Tostig Godwinson, who later faced each other at the Battle of Stamford Bridge.
0 @N6000000001744974577@ NOTE Gytha was a great-granddaughter of Harold I, King of Denmark, her father
1 CONT Thorgils Sprakalegg being a son of Harold's daughter Thyra.
0 @N6000000004533104565@ NOTE <b>Gyda,</b> -1040-, Jarl Godwins Hustru,  var en Datter af ThorgilsSprakalegg og en Søster til Ulf Jarl. Paa et Hærtog i  England skalUlf være bleven hjulpet af Godwin, hvem han derfor tog  i Beskyttelseog anbefalede til sin Svog
1 CONC er Kong Knud. Denne lærte snart hansDygtighed at kjende, og Godwin, som havde ægtet  G. (o. 1019), blevJarl af Wessex (1020) og Kongens særlige  Raadgiver. Godwin var endygtig Kriger, men tillige snild, kundskabsrig
1 CONT og karakterfast; han besad stor Veltalenhed og Evne til at vindeMennesker. G. synes at have været ham jævnbyrdig i Karakter;  hunbesad oprigtig Fromhed og var meget godgjørende mod Kirken  og defattige. En stor Børneflok smykkede 
1 CONC deres Hjem.
1 CONT
1 CONT G. delte sin Ægtefælles vexlende Skæbne. Godwin beholdt  sin Magtunder Hardeknud og under Edvard Bekjender, der ægtede  Godwins DatterEadgyth (1045); Godwin og hans Sønner, der ligeledes havde højeStillinger, vare i nogen Tid Rige
1 CONC ts fornemste Styrere. Menefterhaanden begyndte fransk Indflydelse at gjøre sig
1 CONT gjældende hos Kongen, og da Godwin, støttet af den offentlige Mening,ikke vilde bøje sig for denne, forvistes han af Landet (1051); handrog med sin Familie til Flandern. Godwins Ankomst med en Flaade tilEnglands Kyst og Befolkning
1 CONC ens Tilslutning til ham bevægede Edvard tilat tillade ham at vende tilbage (1052).  Ikke længe efter blev Godwinsyg og døde 15. April 1053. Sønnen  Harald arvede hans Stilling ogIndflydelse og besteg efter Edvards
1 CONT Død Tronen (1066). Men Hertug Vilhelm af Normandie gjorde sine  Kravgjældende, og i Slaget ved Senlac (Hastings) s. A. faldt Harald sammenmed 2 af sine Brødre, Leofwine og Gyrth. G. kom Dagen efter Kampen tilVilhelm og bad om at f
1 CONC aa Haralds Lig udleveret
1 CONT imod at betale dets Vægt i Guld, men hendes Bøn afsloges.  GodwinsParti havde endnu efter Landets Betvingelse stærkt Tilhold i  devestlige Shirer, og under en Opstand 1068 var Exeter, hvor G.  levede,et Midtpunkt for Bevægelsen. B
1 CONC yen maatte overgive sig til Vilhelm, meninden Portene aabnedes, var G. flygtet og søgte Ly  paa nogle Holme iden britiske Kanal. Senere drog hun til Saint  Omer i Flandern. HendesDødsaar er ukjendt. G.s Børn, af hvilke  flere bare
1 CONC  danske Navne, vareSvend, Harald, Tostig, som faldt  ved Stamfordbro 1066, Gyrth,Leofwine, Wulfnoth, Eadgyth, Gunhild, Ælfgifu.
1 CONT
1 CONT Freeman, The Norman conquest of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT <i>Johannes C. H. R. Steenstrup.</i>
0 @N6000000007329555545@ NOTE [Kopi av ROYALS.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Grandniece of Sweyn
0 @N6000000003300434046@ NOTE Sister of Earl Ulf and aunt of Sweyn Estrithson, King of Denmark (1047
0 @N6000000000269777964@ NOTE It is impossible to state exactly the date of Harold's visit to Duke William in Normandy, although it is put at 1064. Probably Harold did make some kind of oath to William, most likely under compulsion. It is certain, however, tha
1 CONC t Harold helped William in a war with the Bretons.
1 CONT
1 CONT On his return he married Ealdgyth, Griffith's widow, even though Edith Swan-neck, who had borne him five children, was still alive. In 1065 the Northumbrians rebelled against Tostig and Harold acquiesced in their choice of Morcar 
1 CONC and Tostig's banishment. In January 1066 King Edward died. Harold, his nominee, was chosen king and crowned in Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke William lost no time in preparing for the invasion of England; and Tostig, after trying the Normans and the Scots, succeeded in drawing Harold Hardrada, King of Norway, to his side. In September the two reached the Humber and
1 CONC  Harold marched to meet them. At Stamford Bridge he won a complete victory on 25 September 1066, Tostig and Harald Hardrada being among the slain. But four days later William landed at Pevensey. Harold marched southwards with the 
1 CONC utmost dispatch and the two armies met at Senlac, about nine miles from Hastings. From nine in the morning, 14 November 1066, the English fought stubbornly until nightfall, when the pretended flight of the Normans drew them from t
1 CONC heir impregnable position and gave the Normans victory. Harold himself fell pierced through the eye with an arrow. His body was recognised by Edith Swan-neck and he was buried at Waltham.
0 @N6000000000437178242@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> Ii, Of England born c. 1020 diedOct. 14, 1066, near Hastings, Sussex, Eng. King of England (1066). known as Harold Godwineson The son of the politically powerful Godwine, earl of Wessex, 
1 CONC he inherited his father's earldom and power in 1053. When Edward the Confessor died in January 1066, Harold's supporters dominated the witan (king's council) and chose him as king. He was opposed by King Harald III Sigurdsson  of 
1 CONC Norway,whom he defeated on Sept. 25, 1066, at Stamford Bridge near York. He then marched south to meet William, duke of Normandy, and was killed at the Battle of Hastings.
0 @N6000000000944948398@ NOTE Konge. Født ca. 1022. Død 14.10.1066 ved Hastings. 
1 CONT Haralds angelsaksiske navn var Godwinson, han var konge av England i 1066.
1 CONT Det var mange liebhabere til den engelske kronen. I sør satt hertug Vilhelm av Normandie og hevdet at han hadde Edvards tilsagn om å få overta hans rike, og paven støttet hans krav. Den som i første omgang gikk av med seieren, var
1 CONC  likevel en innfødt jarl, Harald Gudinesson. Kong Edvard Confessoren overlot ham riket 05.01.1066, da han var døende. Harald ble umiddelbart kronet i Westminster. Paven bannlyste nå Harald for brudd på ed.
1 CONT Harald hadde en misunnelig bror, Tostig eller Toste, som syntes at han hadde like stor rett til kronen. Han søkte nå hjelp hos den norske kongen, og tilbød å støtte ham. Harald Hardråde hadde aldri glemt de arvekrav som også han k
1 CONC unne gjøre gjeldende på Englands trone. Og det var fristende å gripe sjansen. Danmark hadde han - iallfall tilsynelatende - oppgitt; han hadde fred med kong Svein, og inten farer truet derfra. Men ble han herre over både England o
1 CONC g Norge, ville ikke Svein Estridsson ha store mulighetene for å stå seg. Da ville kong Knuts gamle nordsjørike kunne gjenoppstå under norskekongens scepter. Og utsiktene til at en ekspedisjon til England skulle kunne føre fram, va
1 CONC r slett ikke dårlige. Arvekravet ga toget et anstrøk av legitimitet, som nok kunne vinne endel tvilere for hans sak. Han kunne regne med en viss tilslutning i England, særlig i de nordøstlige områdene, der det nordiske islettet va
1 CONC r sterkt. Riktignok viste Toste seg her som en upålitelig informatør, oppslutningen om Harald ble langt dårligere enn det han forespeilet. Men Harald Gudinnesson kunne ikke mobilisere fullt ut mot Harald, han måtte holde et våkent
1 CONC  øye med hertugen av Normandie, som gjorde åpenlyse forberedelser til en landgang.
1 CONT Harald på sin side gjorde også omfattende forberedelser som viser at han regnet med et stort felttog. Han satte seg først fast på Orknøyene, noe som ble desto lettere fordi den mektige Torfinn jarl nylig var død. Her trakk han sam
1 CONC men folk, og her plasserte han sin dronning, den russiske Ellisiv, og hennes to døtre. Så gikk han i land på kysten av Northumbria og vant flere seire i mindre slag, byen York var i ferd med å åpne sine porter for ham, da hanselv 
1 CONC nådde fram. Harald Hardråde lot seg overraske med en mindre avdeling og falt på jordene et stykke utenfor byen, ved Stanford bro den 25.09.1066. Harald Gudinesson selv fulgte ham i døden et par uker senere. Straks etter Harald Har
1 CONC drådes fall måtte han dra i ilmarsj med sine menn sørover for å ta imot normannerne på kanalkysten. Det kom til slag ved Hastings den 14. oktober. Harald ble rammet av en pil i øyet og nedhugget. Liket kjentes igjen av hans elsked
1 CONC e Edgyth Svanehals. Han ble jordet der, men senere flyttet til Waltham kloster.
1 CONT Edgyth var Haralds konkubine. Han ble ca. 1064 gift med Ealdgyth, datter til Elfgar og enke etter Griffits. Dette var et politisk ekteskap.
1 CONT Harald var den siste anglosaksiske konge av England. Normannerhertugen ble hans arvtager.
