0 @N6000000002187757637@ NOTE Edith of Wessex
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Edith of Wessex, (c. 1029 – December 19, 1075), married King Edward the Confessor of England in 1045. The marriage produced no children because Edward took a vow of celibacy. Upon Edward's death, in January 1066, he was succeeded 
1 CONC by Edith's brother, Harold Godwinson.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edith was the daughter of Godwin, Earl of Wessex, one of the most powerful man in England at the time of King Edward's rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her mother Gytha Thorkelsdóttir was daughter to Torkel Styrbjörnsson, granddaughter to Styrbjörn Starke and Tyra and great-granddaughter to both Olof (II) Björnsson and his sister Gyrid by Harold I of Denmark.
0 @N6000000001351716013@ NOTE Sister of Harold Godwinsson (last Anglo-Saxon King of England, slain at the Battle of Hastings)
0 @N6000000000945049105@ NOTE Gyda var gift med Edvard den Gode (Edward Confessor) som var konge 
1 CONT i England fra 1042 til 1066.
1 CONT Hardeknut hadde vært herre over to riker da han døde,  og arvingen
1 CONT etter ham skulle kunne gjøre krav på dem begge. Dette fordi den vanlige
1 CONT oppfatning var at et rike skulle betraktes som kongeættens odel, landet var
1 CONT kongens eiendom. Foreløpig nøyde Magnus Olavsson seg med å gjøre krav på
1 CONT Danmark, og til kong Edvards kroning i 1043 sendte han menn som talte om
1 CONT fred. Sannsynligvis var det for å vinne tid, for da Danmark vel var vunnet,
1 CONT ser det ut til at pipen har fått en annen låt. Iallefall vet vi at kong
1 CONT Edvard hadde en stor flåte ute i 1045 fordi man ventet angrep fra Norge.
1 CONT Engstelsen var stor. Året etter ba Svein Estridsson om bistand fra England,
1 CONT men fikk avslag fordi engelskmennene ikke våget å legge kysten åpen for en
1 CONT mulig invasjon.
1 CONT Legenden beretter også på sin måte om engelskmennenes frykt for
1 CONT nordmennene i denne tiden. Den forteller at kong Edvard en dag midt under
1 CONT gudstjenesten brast i en skrallende latter. De omkringstående trodde han var
1 CONT gått fra forstanden, før han fikk forklart seg: ?Jeg må vel le,? sa kongen,
1 CONT ?nå nettopp så jeg for meg hvorldan kongen av Danmark ramlet overbord og
1 CONT druknet!?
1 CONT I virkeligheten fikk Edvard bli sittende i fred for nordmennene til
1 CONT sin død. Når det ikke ble noe av det norske angrepet, var det uten tvil
1 CONT fordi Svein Estridsson først ga Magnus og siden Harald Hardråde noe å
1 CONT tenke på. Men straks Edvard hadde trukket sitt siste sukk, 05.01.1066,
1 CONT begynte stillingen å forandre seg.
0 @N6000000002187757614@ NOTE Sweyn Godwinson
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Sweyn Godwinson was, in all probability, the older brother of Harold II of England and the first son of Godwin, despite his claims that his father was in fact King Cnut. He was something of a loose cannon and he was eventually exi
1 CONC led, following his murder of his cousin Beorn. He then travelled to Jerusalem, and it was on return from there, according to William of Malmesbury, that he was ambushed by Saracens and mortally wounded. This then left Harold as th
1 CONC e heir apparent of the Godwinson family.
0 @N6000000000437173971@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Earl Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Mercia
0 @N6000000000437637847@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Earl Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Mercia
0 @N6000000000437173984@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Earl of East Anglia
0 @N6000000000437637860@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Earl of East Anglia
0 @N6000000002187757647@ NOTE Gyrth Godwinson
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Gyrth Godwinson was the fourth son of Godwin, and so a younger brother of Harold II of England. He went with his eldest brother Swein into exile to Flanders in 1051. After the death of his father in 1053 he was made an earl. Accor
1 CONC ding to Orderic Vitalis and William of Malmesbury, he tried (ineffectually) to prevent Harold from engaging William of Normandy in battle, saying that he instead could lead the English forces and urging his brother not to break th
1 CONC e oath which had sworn to William confirming the latter's succession. Harold, however, ignored Gyrth's advice. Gyrth fought at and was killed in the Battle of Hastings.
0 @N6000000000437172648@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Earl of Kent
0 @N6000000000437635644@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Earl of Kent
0 @N6000000002187757671@ NOTE Leofwine Godwinson
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Leofwine Godwinson was a younger brother of Harold II of England, the fifth son of Godwin. When the Godwin family was exiled from England in 1051 he went with Harold to Ireland. After the death of his father in 1053 he was made ea
1 CONC rl of Kent. He was killed fighting alongside Harold and Gyrth in the Battle of Hastings.
0 @N6000000002187757682@ NOTE Wulfnoth Godwinson
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Wulfnoth Godwinson was a younger brother of Harold II of England, the sixth son of Godwin. He was given as a hostage to Edward the Confessor when the Godwin family returned from exile in 1052 as an assurance of the family's good b
1 CONC ehaviour and support, and was subsequently handed over to Duke William of Normandy. According to Eadmer's Historia novorum in Anglia, the reason for Harold's excursion to Normandy in 1064/5 was that he wished to free Wulfnoth as w
1 CONC ell as his nephew Hakon. To this end he took with him a vast amount of wealth, all of which was confiscated by Guy of Ponthieu when Harold and his party were shipwrecked. When later Harold swore an oath to William agreeing to beco
1 CONC me his vassal and to support his succession to the English crown, one of the promises made by William in return was that Wulfnoth would be returned safe and sound when William had become king. In fact Harold's assumption of the cr
1 CONC own broke this agreement and Wulfnoth was never released. He died in chains at Salisbury.
