0 @N6000000000945084087@ NOTE Konge av Norge 1066 - 1069, samkonge med sin bror, Olav Kyrre. 
1 CONT Magnus døde i 1069.
1 CONT Hans sønn, Håkon Toresfostre, ble konge i 1093 sammen med sin fetter, Magnus III Olavsson
1 CONT Berrføtt, men døde allerede året etter.
1 CONT Fra Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hardrådes saga:
1 CONT ?101. Kong Magnus Haraldsson rådde for Norge den første vinteren etter kong Haralds fall,
1 CONT og siden styrte han landet i to år sammen med sin bror Olav. Da var det to konger, Magnus
1 CONT hadde nord-delen av landet, og Olav øst-delen. Kong Magnus hadde en sønn som het Håkon.
1 CONT Steigar-Tore fostret ham; han var en uvanlig lovende ung mann. Etter kong Harald
1 CONT Sigurdssons fall mente og sa Svein danekonge, at nå var freden til ende mellom nordmenn og
1 CONT daner, og at freden ikke hadde vært satt for lengre tid enn de begge levde, Harald og Svein.
1 CONT Der var da hæroppbud i begge riker. Haralds sønner hadde almenning av folk og skip ute for
1 CONT Norge, men kong Svein fór sør fra med danehæren. Det fór da sendemenn mellom dem og bar
1 CONT forliksbud. Nordmennene sa at de ville enten holde det samme forlik som var gjort før, eller
1 CONT også kjempe. Derfor ble dette kvedet:
1 CONT Med freds-ord og trusler trygde Olav landet.
1 CONT Det vågde ingen konger å kreve riket.
1 CONT Så sier Stein Herdisson i Olavsdråpa:
1 CONT Den stridsdjerve kongen i Kaupang, mot Svein vil verge sin odel
1 CONT hvor den hellige kongen hviler; en høvding mektig er han.
1 CONT Olav konge vil unne ætten sin hele Norge,
1 CONT krav på landet bør ikke Ulvs arving reise.
1 CONT I denne stevne-leidang ble det gjort forlik mellom kongene og fred mellom landene. Kong
1 CONT Magnus fikk en sykdom, ?revormsott?, og lå en tid. Han døde i Nidaros og ble jordet der. Han
1 CONT var en konge som ble elsket av hele folket.?
0 @N6000000002187915113@ NOTE Magnus II of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Magnus II (1048 - 1069), son of Harald Sigurdsson, was king of Norway from 1066 until 1069.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the late 1050s Magnus led an expedition to the west, to the Northern Isles, the Hebrides, and the Irish Sea. This expedition is only attested in contemporaneous non-Scandinavian sources and is entirely ignored by the sagas. Sin
1 CONC ce the year in which Thorfinn Sigurdsson, Earl of Orkney, died is nowhere recorded with precision—the Orkneyinga saga states that it was in the latter days of Magnus's father—it may be that Magnus's expedition was the occasion on 
1 CONC which Paul and Erlend Thorfinnsson submitted to King Harald. It has been suggested that Magnus also played some part in the war in Scotland in 1057–1058, perhaps supporting Máel Coluim mac Donnchada against Lulach. In 1058 Magnus 
1 CONC and his forces were active in Wales and perhaps in England. According to the Irish Annals of Tigernach, Magnus's goal was to seize power in England, whereas the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle appears to associate the Norwegian fleet with t
1 CONC he return to power of Earl Ælfgar. The Annales Cambriae appear to support the Chronicle version, stating that Magnus Haraldsson laid waste to parts of England in support of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, Ælfgar's son-in-law and ally.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the summer of 1066 King Harald prepared for his ill-fated expedition to England. Before departing, he made his son Magnus regent, and caused him to be crowned King of Norway. Harald's younger son, Olav, accompanied him on the e
1 CONC xpedition. Olav was left in charge of part of his army while Harald advanced. Not expecting opposition, Harold's army traveled light in the warm weather, without the protection of their byrnies. When Harald arrived at Stamford Bri
1 CONC dge the English force fell upon them. Rather than retreating, Harald sent for Olav and his reinforcements. Before Olav's forces arrived the Norse forces were defeated and Harald was fatally wounded. Although a success for the Engl
1 CONC ish, the battle set the stage for the defeat later that summer of England's last Anglo-Saxon king, Harold Godwinson, by William the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT At that time, the Kingdom of Norway was looked upon as a property of the king. As such it would be divided among his heirs, like any other private estate under ancient Norwegian property laws. Retreating from England, Magnus' brot
1 CONC her Olav spent the winter of 1066-1067 in Orkney and returned to Norway in the spring of 1067, claiming his portion of the estate. Magnus was to rule the northern half of the country and Olav the southern. This division is the beg
1 CONC inning of a long series of partitions of the kingdom between the sons and heirs of the ruling king.
1 CONT
1 CONT After just a few years in power, Magnus died in 1069, apparently of ergot poisoning. This left Olav as the sole ruler of Norway.
1 CONT Magnus Haraldsson
1 CONT House of Hardrada
1 CONT Cadet branch of the Fairhair dynasty
1 CONT Born: 1048 Died: 1069
1 CONT Regnal titles
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Harald Hard-rule King of Norway
1 CONT 1066-1069
1 CONT with Olaf Kyrre (1067-1069) Succeeded by
1 CONT O
