0 @N6000000002176666072@ NOTE Having no legitimate son, Robert resolved on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, but
1 CONT died on the return in Nicaea, 1035. The 12-year period after his death was a
1 CONT period of the wildest anarchy. Three of William's Guardians were murdered and
1 CONT relatives kept William in strict concealment, fearing he might experience the
1 CONT same fate. Robert was an unscrupulous and cruel ruler, but energetic and bold.
1 CONT Historical fact during this time: In 1025, Guido de 'Arezzo invents the use
1 CONT of the staff in music.
0 @N6000000002176622272@ NOTE Robert contributed to the restoration of Henry King of France to his
1 CONT throne, and received from the gratitude of that monarch, the Vexin, as
1 CONT an additional to his patrimonial domains. In the 8th year of his reign,
1 CONT curiosity or devotn  induced him to undertake a pilgrimage to the Holy
1 CONT Land, where the fatiges of the journey and the heat of the climate so
1 CONT impaired his consitution he died  on his way home.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Some sources call him Robert I the Magnificent!
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Robert I, duke of Normandy (died 1035), called "the Devil"; father of
1 CONT William the Conqueror; his ferocity and strength subject of medieval
1 CONT legends; aided Edward the Confessor in exile; subject of opera by
1 CONT Meyerbeer ('Robert le Diable').
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
0 @N6000000001956312621@ NOTE
1 CONT Robert The Magnificent, or The Devil, French Robert Le Magnifique, or Le Diableduke of Normandy (1027-35), the younger son of Richard II of Normandy and the father, by his mistress Arlette, of William the Conqueror of England. On 
1 CONC the death of his father (1026/27), Robert contested the duchy with his elder brother Richard III, legally the heir, until the latter's opportune death a few years later. A strong ruler, Robert succeeded in exacting the obedience o
1 CONC f his vassals. On the death of Robert II the Pious, king of France (1031), a crisis arose over the succession to the French throne. The Duke gave his support to Henry I against the party favouring his younger brother; in reward fo
1 CONC r his services he demanded and received the Vexin Français, a territory not far north of Paris. A patron of the monastic reform movement, he died while returning from a pilgrimage to Jerusalem.On the day he left for Turkey, Robert
1 CONC  asked his supporters to accept his illegitimate son William as his officialSuccessor in the event that he didn't manage to return. Some said they would, but many including some of his relatives refused to recognize William,Includ
1 CONC ing Roger "The Spaniard" de Toeni. Who claimed that a bastard had no right to Ducal authority so, he led an armed rebellion against young William and his supporters. (Mistress )Herleve, daughter of Fulbert of Falaise, a tannerA.k.
1 CONC a. Harlette de Falaise
