0 @N6000000002187570240@ NOTE Robert the Magnificent
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert I, called "The Magnificent" (French, "le Magnifique") for his love of finery, and also called "The Devil" was the son of Duke Richard II of Normandy and Judith, daughter of Conan I, Duke of Brittany.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his father died, his elder brother Richard succeeded, whilst he became Count of Hiémois. When Richard died a year later, there were great suspicions that Robert had Richard murdered, hence his other nickname, "Robert le diabl
1 CONC e" (the devil). He is often mis-identified with the legendary Robert the Devil. Robert aided King Henry I of France against Henry's rebellious brother and mother, and for his help he was given the territory of the Vexin. He also i
1 CONC ntervened in the affairs of Flanders, supported Edward the Confessor, who was then in exile at Robert's court, and sponsored monastic reform in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his mistress, Herleva, he was father of two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT the future William the Conqueror. 
1 CONT Adeliza, Countess of Aumale, married three times 
1 CONT Enguerrand II of Ponthieu 
1 CONT Lambert II, Count of Lens 
1 CONT Eudes II of Champagne. 
1 CONT After making his illegitimate son William his heir, he made a pilgrimage to Jerusalem and died at Nicaea between 1-3 of July, 1035. His son William, aged about eight, succeeded him
