1 CONT
1 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Robert contributed to the restoration of Henry King of France to his throne, and received from the gratitude of that monarch, the Vexin, as an addition to his patrimonial domains.  In the 8th year of his reign, curiosit
1 CONC y or devotn induced him to undertake a pilgrimage to the Holy Land, where the fatiges of the journey and the heat of the climate so impaired his consitution he died on his way home.  Some sources call him Robert I the Magnificent.
0 @N6000000003219690911@ NOTE Robert, called "The Magnificent" (French, "le Magnifique") for his love of finery, and also called "The Devil" was the son of Duke Richard II of Normandy and Judith, daughter of Conan I, Duke of Brittany.
1 CONT
1 CONT When his father died, his elder brother Richard succeeded, whilst he became Count of Hiémois. When Richard died a year later, there were great suspicions that Robert had Richard murdered, hence his other nickname, "Robert le diabl
1 CONC e" (the devil). He is sometimes identified with the legendary Robert the Devil. Robert aided King Henry I of France against Henry's rebellious brother and mother, and for his help he was given the territory of the Vexin. He also i
1 CONC ntervened in the affairs of Flanders, supported Edward the Confessor, who was then in exile at Robert's court, and sponsored monastic reform in Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his mistress, Herleva of Falaise, he was father of two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. the future William the Conqueror (1028-1087)
1 CONT    2. Adelaide of Normandy (1030-c. 1083), who was married three times:
1 CONT          1. Enguerrand II, Count of Ponthieu
1 CONT          2. Lambert II, Count of Lens
1 CONT          3. Odo II of Champagne
1 CONT
1 CONT After making his illegitimate son William his heir, he set out on pilgrimage to Jerusalem. According to the Gesta Normannorum Ducum he travelled by way of Constantinople, reached Jerusalem, and died on the return journey at Nicaea
1 CONC  on 2 July 1035. Some sources attribute his death to poison and date it to 1 or 3 July. His son William, aged about eight, succeeded him.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the historian William of Malmesbury, around 1086 William sent a mission to Constantinople and Nicaea, charging it with bringing his father's body back to be buried in Normandy. Permission was granted, but, having trav
1 CONC elled as far as Apulia (Italy) on the return journey, the envoys learned that William himself had meanwhile died. They then decided to re-inter Robert's body in Italy.
0 @N6000000006236142936@ NOTE Robert contributed to the restoration of Henry King of France tohis throne, and received from the gratitude of that monarch, the Vexin, asan additional to his patrimonial domains. In the 8th year of his reign,curiosity or devotion
1 CONC  induced him to undertake a pilgrimage to the Holy Land, where the fatigues of the journey and the heat of the climate so impaired his consitution he died on his way home.
0 @N6000000003178009995@ NOTE DUKE OF THE NORMANS. SUCCEEDE HIS BROTHER RICHARD 11 IN 1027/28. HE DI EDON HIS
1 CONT RETURN FRONM JERUSALEM AT NICAE IN BITHYNIA AND IS BURIED THER ATH THE BASILICA
1 CONT OF ST. MARY'S.
1 CONT
