0 @N6000000002187978974@ NOTE Eystein II of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Øystein Haraldson (born c 1127 apparently in Scotland, died 1157 in Ranfylke, Norway), bastard son of king Harald IV of Norway. Ruled Norway from 1142 together with his brothers Inge I Hunchback and Sigurd the Mouth. His deeds are
1 CONC  recorded in the Heimskringla.
1 CONT
1 CONT The civil wars period of Norwegian history lasted from 1130 to 1240. During this period there were several interlocked conflicts of varying scale and intensity. The background for these conflicts were the unclear Norwegian success
1 CONC ion laws, social conditions and the struggle between Church and King. There were then two main parties, firstly known by varying names or no names at all, but finally condensed into parties of Bagler and Birkebeiner. The rallying 
1 CONC point regularly was a royal son, who was set up as the head figure of the party in question, to oppose the rule of king from the contesting party.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Harald Gille, Eystein's putative father, had been murdered in 1136, leaving his two underage sons as his heirs present in Norwegian soil. Harald had himself come from the British Isles, and had acknowledged that he had left i
1 CONC ssue there. When the teenage Eystein was brought to Norway in c 1142, guardians of his yet younger brothers, the kings, acknowledged him also as a son and heir of the late king Harald.
1 CONT
1 CONT Øystein was captured and killed, fighting against his brother Inge.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married with Ragna Nikolasdottir, a Norwegian gentlewoman.
1 CONT
1 CONT His bastard son Eystein Meyla was later proclaimed king by the Birkebeine party.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Harald IV Gille King of Norway
1 CONT 1136–1157 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Haakon II Herdebrei
0 @N6000000000945053100@ NOTE Eskild var lagmann i Västergötland i 1219. 
1 CONT Hans far, Magnus Minneskiöld, skrives i västgötalagens lagmannslängd til Bjälbo, og
1 CONT var i henhold til denne kilde ?far til Västgötalagmannen Eskil?.
1 CONT Eskild nevnes som vitne i et av kong Knut Erikssons brev. Da Knut døde i 1195, kan
1 CONT Eskild være født omkring 1170, og er neppe sønn til den Ingrid som muligens var Magnus
1 CONT Minneskiölds annen hustru. Han må være meget eldre enn Magnus' sønn Birger jarl som først
1 CONT nevnes på 1230-tallet, så de var antagelig halvbrødre. Eskild førte i sitt segl en løve uten
1 CONT balker eller ginbalker som var en del av seglene til hans halvbror.
1 CONT Eskild nevnes også i 1218 under slittungenes tog til Götaelvens munning som han
1 CONT forgjeves forsøkte å stanse. I den forbindelse mistet han noen skuter han hadde utrustet.
1 CONT I kong Johan Sverkerssons donasjonsbrev fra 1219 nevnes han fremst blandt de
1 CONT verdslige stormennene. Samme sommer besøkte Snorre Sturlasson Eskild.
1 CONT I henhold til et brev av Erik Eriksson, sannsynligvis fra 1225, redigerte han den eldre
1 CONT Västgötalagen.
1 CONT Om Eskild Magnusson anføres i ?Nationalencyklopedin?:
1 CONT ?Eskil (Magnusson), f. ca 1175, d. ca 1227, lagman i Västergötland, son till Magnus
1 CONT Minnesköld och bror till Birger Jarl; gift med Kristina, dotterdotter till Erik den helige och änka
1 CONT efter den norske jarlen Håkon Folkvidsson. E. uppträdde som medlare i konflikter mellan de
1 CONT norska och svenska kungarna. Han tillskrivs initiativet till nedteckningen av äldre
1 CONT Västgötalagen. E. besöktes 1219 av historieskrivaren Snorre Sturlasson, och dennes uppgifter
1 CONT om svenska samhällsförhållanden torde i stor utsträckning ha E. som källa?.
0 @N6000000006127688347@ NOTE Eskil Magnusson, född cirka 1170-1175 och död omkring 1227, var son till Magnus Minnisköld i dennes första kända äktenskap och således halvbror till Birger jarl. Han gifte sig omkring 1217 med Kristina Nilsdotter, som var dotterdo
1 CONC tter till Erikden helige och änka efter den norske jarlen Hakon galen.Eskil Magnusson framträder i skriftliga dokument redan i slutet av Knut Erikssons regeringstid. Han var lagman i Västergötland senast från 1217. Eskil var enlig
1 CONC t Västergötlands lagmanslängd författare till Västgötalagen. Under 1220-talet första årdeltog han i förhandlingar med den norske kungen Håkon Håkonsson gällande Ribbungarnas användande av Värmland som basområde för plundringståg i
1 CONC n i Norge.Baneret som kung Erik Knutsson av Sverige fört i slaget på Gestilren förvarades hos Eskil Magnusson i Skara, som år 1219 överlämnade det som hedersgåva till den gästande isländske lagsagesmannen Snorre Sturlasson.
0 @N6000000002188021002@ NOTE Eskil Magnusson
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eskil Magnusson was the lawspeaker of Västergötland c. 1215–1227. He was the son of Magnus Minniskiöld, and the elder brother of Birger Jarl, one of the most powerful men of his time in Scandinavia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Due to the location of his jurisdiction and his marriage to Kristina, the widow of the Norwegian earl Hakon the Mad (d. 1214), Eskil had good contacts in Norway and often functioned as a negotiator between the Swedish and the Norw
1 CONC egian government. In 1218, he was visited by his Icelandic colleague Snorri Sturluson.
1 CONT
1 CONT An old list of the lawspeakers of Västergötland tells that he collected and edited the provice's laws, and administered justice with great consideration. He had good judgment, the learning of a cleric and he was superior to all th
1 CONC e chiefains of the kingdom. He also distinguished himself in bravery and the list claims that it would take a long time until another man of that kind was born.
