0 @N6000000001351708738@ NOTE Fought in battle in 1008 at Corham with Uchtred, son of Waltheof, Earl of Northumberland; overcame the Danes in 1017; published a code of laws.   In 1016 made his grandson Duncan King of Strathclyde and named him his successor.  D
1 CONC efeated Northumbrians at Carham on Tweed & conquered Lothian in 1005.  Controlled beyond old Alba of the Angles in the South East, and Britions in South West. Said to have married an Irishwoman from Ossory.  Killed by his kinsman.
0 @N6000000002319315097@ NOTE conquered Lothian 1018. King of Alba, King of Strathclyde.
1 CONT      said to have married an Irishwoman from Ossory.
1 CONT      Killed by his kinsman.
1 CONT
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT  In 1018, under MacAlpin's descendant Malcolm II, the Angles were
1 CONT finally
1 CONT  defeated in this northerly part of Britain and Lothian came under
1 CONT Scottish rule.
1 CONT  In the same year, the British (Celtic) King of Strathclyde died
1 CONT leaving no heir;
1 CONT  his throne went to Malcolm's grandson Duncan. In 1034, Duncan became
1 CONT King
1 CONT  of a much-expanded Scotland that included Pict-land, Scotland, Lothian,
1 CONT  Cumbria and Strathclyde. It excluded large tracts in the north, the
1 CONT Shetlands,
1 CONT  Orkneys and the Western Isles, which were held by the Scandinavians.
1 CONT There
1 CONT  was still no established boundary between Scotland and England.
0 @N6000000002187590678@ NOTE Malcolm II of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Malcolm II
1 CONT (Máel Coluim mac Cináeda) 
1 CONT King of the Scots 
1 CONT
1 CONT Fanciful and anachronistic 17th century depiction of the king. It bears no relationship to the king's actual appearance. 
1 CONT Reign 1005–1034 
1 CONT Born c. 980 
1 CONT Died 25 November 1034 
1 CONT  Glamis 
1 CONT Buried Iona 
1 CONT Predecessor Kenneth III (Cináed mac Duib) 
1 CONT Successor Duncan I (Donnchad mac Crínáin) 
1 CONT Issue Bethóc, other daughters 
1 CONT Royal House Alpin 
1 CONT Father Kenneth II (Cináed mac Maíl Coluim) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Máel Coluim mac Cináeda (Modern Gaelic: Maol Chaluim mac Choinnich)[1], known in modern anglicized regnal lists as Malcolm II, and nicknamed Forranach, "the Destroyer", [2] (c. 980–25 November 1034), was King of the Scots from 100
1 CONC 5 until his death.[3] He was a son of Kenneth II (Cináed mac Maíl Coluim); the Prophecy of Berchán says that his mother was a woman of Leinster and refers to him as Máel Coluim Forranach, "the destroyer".[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT To the Irish annals which recorded his death, Malcolm was ard rí Alban, High King of Scotland. In the same way that Brian Bóruma, High King of Ireland, was very far from being the only king in Ireland, Malcolm was one of several k
1 CONC ings within the geographical boundaries of modern Scotland: his fellow kings included the king of Strathclyde, who ruled much of the south-west, various Norse-Gael kings of the western coasts and the Hebrides and, nearest and most
1 CONC  dangerous rivals, the Kings or Mormaers of Moray. To the south, in the kingdom of England, the Earls of Bernicia and Northumbria, whose predecessors as kings of Northumbria had once ruled most of southern Scotland, still controll
1 CONC ed large parts of the south-east.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early Years 
1 CONT 2 Bernicia 
1 CONT 3 Canute 
1 CONT 4 Orkney and Moray 
1 CONT 5 Strathclyde and the succession 
1 CONT 6 Death and posterity 
1 CONT 7 Notes 
1 CONT 8 References 
1 CONT 9 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early Years
1 CONT In 997, the killer of Constantine III (Causantín mac Cuilén) is credited as being Cináed mac Maíl Coluim, "Kenneth son of Malcolm". Since there is no known and relevant Cináed mac Maíl Coluim alive at that time (Kenneth II, son of
1 CONC  Malcolm I, having died in 995), it is considered an error for either Kenneth, son of Dub (Cináed mac Duib), who succeeded Constantine as Kenneth III, or, possibly, Malcolm himself, the son of Kenneth II. [6] Whether Malcolm kille
1 CONC d Constantine or not, there is no doubt that in 1005 he killed Constantine's successor Kenneth III in battle at Monzievaird in Strathearn.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT John of Fordun writes that Malcolm defeated a Norwegian army "in almost the first days after his coronation", but this is not reported elsewhere. Fordun says that the Bishopric of Mortlach (later moved to Aberdeen) was founded in 
1 CONC thanks for this victory over the Norwegians, but this claim appears to have no foundation.[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Bernicia
1 CONT The first reliable report of Malcolm's reign is of an invasion of Bernicia, perhaps the customary crech ríg (literally royal prey, a raid by a new king made to demonstrate prowess in war), which involved a siege of Durham. This ap
1 CONC pears to have resulted in a heavy defeat, by the Northumbrians led by Uchtred the Bold, later Earl of Bernicia, which is reported by the Annals of Ulster.[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT A second war in Bernicia, probably in 1018, was more successful. The Battle of Carham, by the River Tweed, was a victory for the Scots led by Malcolm and the men of Strathclyde led by their king, Eógan II (Owen the Bald). By this 
1 CONC time Earl Uchtred may have been dead, and Eric of Norway (Eiríkr Hákonarson) was appointed Earl of Northumbria by his brother-in-law Canute the Great, although his authority seems to have been limited to the south, the former king
1 CONC dom of Deira, and he took no action against the Scots so far as is known.[10] The work De obsessione Dunelmi (The siege of Durham, associated with Symeon of Durham) claims that Uchtred's brother Eadwulf Cudel surrendered Lothian t
1 CONC o Malcolm, presumably in the aftermath of the defeat at Carham. This is likely to have been the lands between Dunbar and the Tweed as other parts of Lothian had been under Scots control before this time. It has been suggested that
1 CONC  Canute received tribute from the Scots for Lothian, but as he had likely received none from the Bernician Earls this is not very probable.[11]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Canute
1 CONT Canute, reports the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, led an army into Scotland on his return from pilgrimage to Rome. The Chronicle dates this to 1031, but there are reasons to suppose that it should be dated to 1027.[12] Burgundian chronic
1 CONC ler Rodulfus Glaber recounts the expedition soon afterwards, describing Malcolm as "powerful in resources and arms ... very Christian in faith and deed."[13] Ralph claims that peace was made between Malcolm and Canute through the 
1 CONC intervention of Richard, Duke of Normandy, brother of Canute's wife Emma. Richard died in about 1027 and Rodulfus wrote close in time to the events.[14]
1 CONT
1 CONT It has been suggested that the root of the quarrel between Canute and Malcolm lies in Canute's pilgrimage to Rome, and the coronation of Holy Roman Emperor Conrad II, where Canute and Rudolph III, King of Burgundy had the place of
1 CONC  honour. If Malcolm were present, and the repeated mentions of his piety in the annals make it quite possible that he made a pilgrimage to Rome, as did Macbeth (Mac Bethad mac Findláich) in later times, then the coronation would h
1 CONC ave allowed Malcolm to publicly snub Canute's claims to overlordship.[15]
1 CONT
1 CONT Canute obtained rather less than previous English kings, a promise of peace and friendship rather than the promise of aid on land and sea that Edgar and others had obtained. The sources say that Malcolm was accompanied by one or t
1 CONC wo other kings, certainly Macbeth, and perhaps Echmarcach mac Ragnaill, King of Mann and the Isles, and of Galloway.[16] The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle remarks of the submission "but he [Malcolm] adhered to that for only a little while
1 CONC ".[17] Canute was soon occupied in Norway against Olaf Haraldsson and appears to have had no further involvement with Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Orkney and Moray
1 CONT A daughter of Malcolm, whose name is not known, married Sigurd Hlodvisson, Earl of Orkney.[18] Their son Thorfinn Sigurdsson was said to be five years old when Sigurd was killed on 23 April 1014 in the Battle of Clontarf. The Orkn
1 CONC eyinga Saga says that Thorfinn was raised at Malcolm's court and was given the Mormaerdom of Caithness by his grandfather. Thorfinn, says the Heimskringla, was the ally of the king of Scots, and counted on Malcolm's support to res
1 CONC ist the "tyranny" of Norwegian King Olaf Haraldsson.[19] The chronology of Thorfinn's life is problematic, and he may have had a share in the Earldom of Orkney while still a child, if he was indeed only five in 1014.[20] Whatever 
1 CONC the exact chronology, before Malcolm's death a client of the king of Scots was in control of Caithness and Orkney, although, as with all such relationships, it is unlikely to have lasted beyond his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT If Malcolm exercised control over Moray, which is far from being generally accepted, then the annals record a number of events pointing to a struggle for power in the north. In 1020, Macbeth's father Findláech mac Ruaidrí was kill
1 CONC ed by the sons of his brother Máel Brigte.[21] It seems that Máel Coluim mac Máil Brigti (Malcolm, son of Máel Brigte) took control of Moray, for his death is reported in 1029.[22]
1 CONT
1 CONT It should be said that whatever the Irish annals say, English and Scandinavian writers appear to see Macbeth as the rightful king of Moray: witness their descriptions of the meeting with Canute in 1027, before the death of Máel Co
1 CONC luim mac Máil Brigti. Máel Coluim was followed as king or mormaer by his brother Gille Coemgáin, husband of Gruoch, a granddaughter of King Kenneth III. It has been supposed that Macbeth was responsible for the killing of Gille Co
1 CONC emgáin in 1032, but if Macbeth had a cause for feud in the killing of his father in 1020, Malcolm too had reason to see Gille Coemgáin dead. Not only had Gille Coemgáin's ancestors killed many of Malcolm's kin, but Gille Coemgáin 
1 CONC and his son Lulach might be rivals for the throne. Malcolm had no living sons, and the threat to his plans for the succession was obvious. The following year another man who might some day be king, Gruoch's brother or nephew, was 
1 CONC killed by Malcolm.[23]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Strathclyde and the succession
1 CONT It has traditionally been supposed that King Eógan the Bald of Strathclyde died at the Battle of Carham and that the kingdom passed into the hands of the Scots afterwards. This rests on some very weak evidence. It is far from cert
1 CONC ain that Eógan died at Carham, and it is reasonable certain that there were kings of Strathclyde as late as the 1054, when Edward the Confessor sent Earl Siward to install "Máel Coluim son of the king of the Cumbrians". The confus
1 CONC ion is old, probably inspired by William of Malmesbury and embellished by John of Fordun, but there is no firm evidence that the kingdom of Strathclyde was a part of the kingdom of the Scots, rather than a loosely subjected kingdo
1 CONC m, before the time of Malcolm II of Scotland's great-grandson Malcolm III (Máel Coluim mac Donnchada).[24]
1 CONT
1 CONT By the 1030s Malcolm's sons, if he had had any, were dead. The only evidence that he did have a son or sons is in Rodulfus Glaber's chronicle where Canute is said to have stood as godfather to a son of Malcolm.[25] His grandson Th
1 CONC orfinn would have been unlikely to accepted as king by the Scots, and he chose the sons of his other daughter, Bethóc, who was married to Crínán, lay abbot of Dunkeld, and perhaps Mormaer of Atholl. It may be no more than coincide
1 CONC nce, but in 1027 the Irish annals had reported the burning of Dunkeld, although no mention is made of the circumstances.[26] Malcolm's chosen heir, and the first tánaise ríg certainly known in Scotland, was Duncan (Donnchad mac Cr
1 CONC ínáin).
1 CONT
1 CONT It is possible that a third daughter of Malcolm married Findláech mac Ruaidrí and that Macbeth was thus his grandson, but this rests on relatively weak evidence.[27]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death and posterity
1 CONT Malcolm died in 1034, Marianus Scotus giving the date as 25 November 1034. The king lists say that he died at Glamis, variously describing him as a "most glorious" or "most victorious" king. The Annals of Tigernach report that "Má
1 CONC el Coluim mac Cináeda, king of Scotland, the honour of all the west of Europe, died." The Prophecy of Berchán, perhaps the inspiration for John of Fordun and Andrew of Wyntoun's accounts where Malcolm is killed fighting bandits, s
1 CONC ays that he died by violence, fighting "the parricides", suggested to be the sons of Máel Brigte of Moray.[28]
1 CONT
1 CONT Perhaps the most notable feature of Malcolm's death is the account of Marianus, matched by the silence of the Irish annals, which tells us that Duncan I became king and ruled for five years and nine months. Given that his death in
1 CONC  1040 is described as being "at an immature age" in the Annals of Tigernach, he must have been a young man in 1034. The absence of any opposition suggests that Malcolm had dealt thoroughly with any likely opposition in his own lif
1 CONC etime.[29]
1 CONT
1 CONT On the question of Malcolm's putative pilgrimage, pilgrimages to Rome, or other long-distance journeys, were far from unusual. Thorfinn Sigurdsson, Canute and Macbeth have already been mentioned. Rognvald Kali Kolsson is known to 
1 CONC have gone crusading in the Mediterranean in the 12th century. Nearer in time, Domnall mac Eógain of Strathclyde died on pilgrimage to Rome in 975 as did Máel Ruanaid uá Máele Doraid, King of the Cenél Conaill, in 1025.
1 CONT
1 CONT Not a great deal is known of Malcolm's activities beyond the wars and killings. The Book of Deer records that Malcolm "gave a king's dues in Biffie and in Pett Meic-Gobraig, and two davochs" to the monastery of Old Deer.[30] As ha
1 CONC s been said, he was probably not the founder of the Bishopric of Mortlach-Aberdeen. John of Fordun has a peculiar tale to tell, related to the supposed "Laws of Malcom MacKenneth", saying that Malcolm gave away all of Scotland, ex
1 CONC cept for the Moot Hill at Scone, which is unlikely to have the least basis in fact.[31]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Máel Coluim mac Cináeda is the Mediaeval Gaelic form. 
1 CONT ^ Skene, Chronicles, pp. 99-100. 
1 CONT ^ Malcolm's birth date is not known, but must have been around 980 if the Flateyjarbók is right in dating the marriage of his daughter and Sigurd Hlodvisson to the lifetime of Olaf Tryggvason; Early Sources, p. 528, quoting Olaf T
1 CONC ryggvason's Saga. 
1 CONT ^ Early Sources, pp. 574–575. 
1 CONT ^ Higham, pp. 226–227, notes that the kings of the English had neither lands nor mints north of the Tees. 
1 CONT ^ Early Sources, pp. 517–518. John of Fordun has Malcolm as the killer; Duncan, p. 46, credits Cináed mac Duib (i.e. King Kenneth III) with the death of Constantine. 
1 CONT ^ Chronicon Scotorum, s.a. 1005; Early Sources, pp. 521–524; Fordun, IV, xxxviii. Berchán places Cináed's death by the Earn. 
1 CONT ^ Early Sources, p. 525, note 1; Fordun, IV, xxxix–xl. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, pp.27–28; Smyth, pp.236–237; Annals of Ulster, s.a. 1006. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, pp. 28–29 suggests that Earl Uchtred may not have died until 1018. Fletcher accepts that he died in Spring 1016 and the Eadwulf Cudel was Earl of Bernicia when Carham was fought in 1018; Higham, pp.225–230, agrees. Smyth
1 CONC , pp. 236–237 reserves judgement as to the date of the battle, 1016 or 1018, and whether Uchtred was still living when it was fought. See also Stenton, pp.418–419. 
1 CONT ^ Early Sources, p. 544, note 6; Higham, pp. 226–227. 
1 CONT ^ ASC, Ms D, E and F; Duncan, pp. 29–30. 
1 CONT ^ Early Sources, pp. 545–546. 
1 CONT ^ Ralph was writing in 1030 or 1031; Duncan, p. 31. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, pp.31–32; the alternative, he notes, that Canute was concerned about support for Olaf Haraldsson, "is no better evidenced." 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, pp.29–30. St. Olaf's Saga, c. 131 says "two kings came south from Fife in Scotland" to meet Canute, suggesting only Malcolm and Macbeth, and that Canute returned their lands and gave them gifts. That Echmarcach was king 
1 CONC of Galloway is perhaps doubtful; the Annals of Ulster record the death of Suibne mac Cináeda, rí Gall-Gáedel ("King of Galloway") by Tigernach, in 1034. 
1 CONT ^ ASC, Ms. D, s.a. 1031. 
1 CONT ^ Early Sources, p. 528; Orkneyinga Saga, c. 12. 
1 CONT ^ Orkneyinga Saga, cc. 13–20 & 32; St. Olaf's Saga, c. 96. 
