1 CONT           1161-1184. 
1 CONT           Sigurd Jorsalfare hadde også sønnen Magnus "Blinde" Sigurdsson (konge i Norge i
1 CONT           årene 1130-1135) , hvis mor var Borghild Olavsdatter, datter av Olav Dal, fra
1 CONT           Store-Dal i Borge i Østfold. Som barn ble Magnus "Blinde" sendt bort til fostring hos
1 CONT           Vidkunn Jonssen på Bjarkøy i Hålogaland. Sigurd Jorsalfare ble skilt fra Malmfrid,
1 CONT           og hun ble igjen gift med den danske kongen Erik Emune, sønn av Erik Eiegod. 
1 CONT           Sigurd Jorsalfare ble annen gang gift med Cecilia, datter av en mektig mann, og
1 CONT           kongen skal visstnok ha holdt mye av henne. Men presteskapet var sterkt imot dette
1 CONT           giftemålet. 
1 CONT           Sigurd Jorsalfare seilte høsten 1108 - med 60 skip og 10000 mann - fra Hordaland
1 CONT           mot Jerusalem, og det gikk tre år før han kom tilbake til Norge. På sin ferd til
1 CONT           Jerusalem var han stadig i strid med hedenske vikingflåter, blant annet kjempet han
1 CONT           mot maurerne på Pyreneer-halvøya. På veien hjem til Norge gikk de i land i
1 CONT           Konstantinopel, hvor Sigurd Jorsalfare forærte keiseren sine skip. Derfra tok de
1 CONT           landeveien gjennom Ungarn, Tyskland og Danmark. 
1 CONT           Med stor iver tok Sigurd Jorsalfare seg av kirkens anliggende, og Stavanger
1 CONT           bispedømme ble opprettet. 
1 CONT           Han er den eneste av de gamle norske konger som det fremdeles finnes levninger
1 CONT           etter - hodeskallen er bevart på Akershus.
0 @N6000000004533031225@ NOTE Afrika/Alfreka, sister of Duncan Earl of Fife.  [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT ---------------------
1 CONT He [Harald Maddadsson] m., 1stly, Alfreka, dau. of Duncan, 4th Earl ofFife (1136-1154), Regent of Scotland 1153, but repudiated her(probably after the death of her father) during Jarl Kali-Ragnvald’sabsence on pilgrimage, having h
1 CONC ad issue.   [Complete Peerage,X:Appendix A:22-25]
1 CONT ---------------------
1 CONT I originally interpreted BP to indicate that she was sister of Duncan,4th Earl of Fife, but the following post-em from Curt Hofemann,curt_hofemann@yahoo.com, might clear up the conflict:
1 CONT
1 CONT Burke’s & CP were not at odds, but were both correct as flws:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. Gillemichal, 3rd Earl of Fife, d. bef Jul 1136
1 CONT 11. Duncan, 4th Earl of Fife, d. 1154.  Regent for Malcolm IV “theMaiden” K of Scots until his majority 1154.
1 CONT 111. Duncan 5th Earl of Fife, d. 1203. Justiciar of Scotland, m. Ela(NN).
1 CONT 112. Afrika/Alfreka/Aufrica
1 CONT
1 CONT Also see: CP I:305, ES II:89, ES III:356, Paget p17 & 153, SP-Fife,Watney 31, 234 & 388.
1 CONT
1 CONT Several websites with limited but pertinent info:
1 CONT http://www.stirnet.com/HTML/genie/british/ff/fife1.htm
1 CONT which cites SP (calling it TSP)
1 CONT http://www.tartans.com/official/MacDuff/usa/chief.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT And from the Gen-Medieval archives:
1 CONT http://groups.google.com/groups?q=gillemichael+fife+group:soc.genealogy.*&hl=en&lr=&ie=UTF-8&group=soc.genealogy.*&selm=2a47b805.362ffa06%40aol.com& rnum=1
1 CONT
1 CONT Regards,
1 CONT Curt
0 @N6000000004533031408@ NOTE Gorm(f)la(e)th/Hvafleda/Harlod/Hvorflod, daughter of Malcolm MacHeth,1st Earl of Ross, supposedly himself son of Angus Earl of Moray.[Burke's Peerage]
0 @N6000000002187914365@ NOTE Somerled
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Somerled was a military and political leader of the Scottish Isles in the 12th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Somerled Rex Insularum.
1 CONT
1 CONT Somerled (English) - Sumarlidi (Norse) - Somhairle (Scottish)
1 CONT
1 CONT Somerled first appears in historical chronicles in the year 1140 as the regulus, or King, of Kintyre ( Cinn Tìre ) when he marries Raghnailt the daughter of the King of Man. 1143 saw the deaths of two kings: David I of Scotland an
1 CONC d Amhlaibh of Man. There was much confusion and discord as a result and Somerled took his chance - making offensive moves against both Scotland and Man under his brother in law Goraidh mac Amhlaibh.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is likely that events occurred as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT A summoning was sent to Somerled Dougal - Somerled's own son by his wife, the daughter of the Manx King - to move so he might be "King over the Isles". In 1156 Goraidh was defeated in battle against 80 ships of Somerleds fleet and
1 CONC  the two enemies parititioned the isles between them. Goraidh kept the islands noth of Ardnamurchan with Somerled gaining the rest. However, two years following this Somerled returned do the Isle of Man with 53 warships. He defeat
1 CONC ed Goraidh again and this time forced him to flee to Norway. Somerleds kingdom now stretched from the Isle of Man to the Butt of Lewis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thus both Viking and Scot formed one people under a single lord and came to share a single culture, one way of life - they were to become a powerful and noted race known as the Gall-Gaidheal, literally meaning 'Foreign-Gaels'. It 
1 CONC was upon the seas their power was situated under the rule of the kings of the isles yet new enemies arose in the East. The Stewarts made inroads in the west coast and eventually Somerled assembled a sizeable army to repel them. He
1 CONC  advanced to the centre of Stewarts own territory , to Renfrew, where a great battle was fought in 1164. Much confusion surrounds the manner of the battle, whether a battle occurred at all, but what is certain is that Somerled was
1 CONC  killed although how is also unsure. What is certain is that following the death of their great leader his army was greatly dispirited and retreated from the area.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the death of Somerled several powerful lords emerged from within his kingdom. The lordship was contested by two main families ; that of Somerled and his descendants and that of the descendants of Goraidh mac Amhlaibh. Du
1 CONC ring the 12th and 13th centuries the Scandinavian world saw much change in methods of rule and administration which ultimately resulted in more strongly centralized, unified kingdoms such as Denmark and Norway. However this did no
1 CONC t happen in the Kingdom of the Isles which was instead absorbed into the greater Kingdom of Scotland albeit its place in that state and the loyalty of its inhabitants to the King of Scots would remain peripheral and temperamental 
1 CONC for centuries to come.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT Arguably Somerled's greatest legacy was the explusion of the Norse from their Scottish foothold along the west coast - a foothold they had maintained for centuries. While the successor Lords of the Isles remained of questionable l
1 CONC oyalty with regard to the Scottish Crown and nominally under the sovereignty of Norway they were Scottish in language and culture rather than Norse and foreign. As a result of this 'Gaelic Renaissance' under Somerled the great dic
1 CONC hotomy which later formed in Scotland was between the Gaelic Scots (who came over the following centuries to be generally restricted to the Highlands and Hebrides) and the Lowland Anglo-Scots who, with the displacement of Scottish
1 CONC  Gaelic by Inglis - later renamed Scots - across the lowlands came to dominate the east coast and south of the country.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Stiùbhart, Domhnall Uilleam Rìoghachd nan Eilean (Clò Hallaig, 2005) ISBN 0-9549914-0-0
1 CONT     * MacPhee, Kathleen Somerled:Hammer of the Norse (NWP, 2004) ISBN 1-903238-24-2
1 CONT     * Williams, Ronald The Lords of the Isles (Chatto & Windus, 1997) ISBN 1-899863-17-6
0 @N6000000003635727213@ NOTE [2706] WSHNGT.ASC file (Geo Washington Ahnentafel) # 545340
